    {
      slug: 'how-long-to-build-a-website',
      title: "How Long Does It Take to Build a Small Business Website?",
      category: 'Strategy',
      date: '02 Nov 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Reancirl Balaba',
      authorRole: 'Co-Founder',
      authorPhoto: '',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1498050108023-c5249f4df085?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "How long does it take to build a website for a small business? A packaged build can launch in 7 days. Custom builds typically take longer.",
      content: `
        <p>How long does it take to build a website for a small business? For a straightforward site with a packaged build, the answer is 7 business days — provided your content is ready on day one and you give feedback within 24 hours at each review stage. Custom builds with booking systems, portals or e-commerce typically take between 5 and 12 weeks, depending on scope.</p>
        <h2>How long does a website build actually take?</h2>
        <p>The single biggest factor is scope. A five-page brochure site — a homepage, service pages, an about page and a contact form — is a fundamentally different project from an eight-page site with an online booking system, payment integration and a client dashboard. Add those features and the timeline grows, because design, development and testing all take proportionally longer.</p>
        <p>Content readiness is the second factor, and it is almost entirely in your hands. A studio cannot design around text that does not exist yet. If you start a project without finalised copy, photos and a logo, the timeline shifts — not because the studio is slow, but because work pauses while you gather your materials. Studios that consistently hit short timelines front-load the content collection onto day one.</p>
        <p>The third factor is feedback speed. Every build has one or two review stages where you check what has been done and request changes. Giving clear, consolidated feedback within 24 hours keeps the project moving. Fragmented feedback — a few notes today, more tomorrow, then a rethink the following week — is the most common reason websites take months instead of weeks.</p>
        <h2>The 7-business-day build</h2>
        <p>A packaged 7-day website build is not a rushed template job. It is a professionally designed website delivered on a short, predictable timeline by a studio that runs the same process every time. The efficiency comes from the process, not from cutting corners on quality.</p>
        <p>At Fazed Digital, <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/lp/7-day-website">our 7-day website package</a> launches in 7 business days when you complete the day-one brief and give feedback within 24 hours at each review. If we miss the deadline on our side, you receive 20% off the project fee. Packages include 2 revision rounds on Starter, 3 on Growth, and unlimited on Pro. You own the domain, the site files and all the content once complete — there is no lock-in contract.</p>
        <p>Pricing starts from $499 USD, or ₱19,999 in the Philippines. Extra pages are $79 each (₱2,500 in the Philippines), and you know the price upfront before work begins.</p>
        <h2>When a longer timeline is realistic</h2>
        <p>Not every website fits a 7-day package, and a good studio will tell you that plainly. Custom builds with multiple user roles, third-party integrations, booking engines or admin portals are discovery-led projects. A brief phase is needed to scope the work properly, and the build itself will take weeks, not days.</p>
        <p>Our case studies give a sense of what longer timelines look like in practice. A brand identity plus website for Cleen & Green, a cleaning company in the Philippines, was completed in 8 weeks. A full platform with multiple user types — the CrateOnScene PWA for a US automotive events client — shipped in 5 weeks. The scope, not an arbitrary schedule, sets the pace. Our <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/blog/fixed-price-website-design">guide to fixed-price website design</a> explains the difference between a packaged and a bespoke build, and what each includes.</p>
        <h2>What causes delays</h2>
        <p>In practice, most website projects run late because of things that happen before the first line of code is written. Unclear briefs that get revised once the design is done. Stakeholders who need to approve content but are not looped in from the start. Photography scheduled after the build begins rather than before it. None of these are the studio's fault, and none of them are hard to avoid with a small amount of upfront planning.</p>
        <p>If you have not yet collected your logo files, your service descriptions and at least six to ten photos of your work or premises, do that before you engage a studio. A studio that asks for these materials on day one is doing you a favour, not slowing you down.</p>
        <p>Some of this can be avoided by asking your studio for a content brief before you begin. A simple document listing what text, images and information each page needs — written at the start of the project, not the day before a deadline — gives you enough time to gather everything properly. The more thoroughly you prepare, the closer to the 7-day timeline you will land.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>How long does a 5-page website take to build?</strong> With a packaged build and content ready on day one, a five-page site can launch in 7 business days. Without ready content, or with slow feedback, the same project typically takes 3 to 4 weeks.</p>
        <p><strong>Can I really get a website built in a week?</strong> Yes — with the right process. You need to send your content on day one, give feedback within 24 hours at each review, and be working with a studio that has built that workflow deliberately. A standard hourly-rate studio rarely delivers in under 3 weeks because the project management structure does not support it.</p>
        <p><strong>What slows down a website project the most?</strong> Missing content. Most delays come from the client side — copy not written, photos not taken, approvals not given. The studio cannot do its job until you do yours first.</p>
