        <p>When a developer or general contractor is shortlisting subcontractors, they often send your site link to a colleague or project manager. That person spends roughly thirty seconds deciding whether to take the conversation further. A portfolio with clear photos, project location, and a short note on scale and scope does that job quickly. A handful of blurry images with no context does not.</p>
        <p>A Framing Company is a Utah-based framing contractor we worked with in 2024. Their previous site had no portfolio presence, so they were losing bids to competitors who looked more credible online. We built a WordPress site structured around three core audiences — general contractors, developers and homeowners — with service pages for wood framing, concrete and roofing, a project gallery of completed Utah builds, and client testimonials throughout. After launch, the team reported inbound enquiries from contractors and developers who found them through search or were sent the link during the bidding process. You can read more on <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/work/01">the A Framing Company case page</a>.</p>
