        <h2>What drives the price of an ecommerce website</h2>
        <p>Four things have the biggest impact on cost: the number of products, the payment gateways you need to connect, the level of custom development, and which platform the store runs on. A catalogue of ten products with a straightforward checkout is a very different build from a store with a thousand SKUs, per-customer pricing, and a connected inventory system.</p>
        <p>Payment gateway integration adds both upfront development work and ongoing transaction fees. In the Philippines, the common options are GCash, Maya, PayMongo, and Xendit, each with its own integration requirements and fee structure. A store that needs two or three payment methods configured and tested will cost more to build than one with a single gateway.</p>
        <p>Custom features — product filters, bulk order forms, loyalty schemes, address books, and calculations for different regions — add to the build cost. So does design. A site built from a ready-made theme with minor adjustments costs less than one designed from scratch to match your brand exactly.</p>
        <h2>Platform builds versus custom development</h2>
        <p>For most small businesses in the Philippines, a platform-based store — WooCommerce on WordPress, or PrestaShop — is the practical starting point. These platforms handle the core ecommerce logic (product pages, cart, checkout, order management) out of the box. A developer configures and extends them for your catalogue and payment methods. The result is a manageable, client-editable store without the cost of building those features from scratch.</p>
        <p>A custom-built store makes sense when your requirements do not fit any platform's standard workflow. A store that also manages a rental fleet, connects to a warehouse system, or handles B2B order approvals from multiple buyers is likely better served by custom development than by an increasingly complex platform configuration. The tradeoff is higher upfront cost and a longer build timeline.</p>
        <p>For a closer look at how the platform decision plays out, the post on <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/blog/wordpress-vs-custom-build">WordPress versus a custom build</a> covers when each approach works and where the cost differences come from.</p>
