        <p>A care plan covers security updates, platform upgrades, and regular backups. For an ecommerce store, skipping maintenance carries a higher risk than it would for a static site: a plugin conflict or a security gap can take a live checkout offline. Our care plans start from ₱3,500 per month and include regular updates and monitoring.</p>
        <h2>Ecommerce website prices in the Philippines: what to expect</h2>
        <p>Agency price guides from directories such as GoodFirms show that ecommerce development rates vary widely in the Philippines, with the range driven by complexity and the experience of the team. A simple platform-based store sits at the lower end; a fully custom-built platform with multiple integrations is significantly higher.</p>
        <p>Our seven-day packages start from ₱19,999 for a five-page site and ₱34,999 for an eight-page build. These cover standard small business sites. Ecommerce functionality — payment integrations, a product catalogue, an order management workflow — falls under our custom development work, which we scope and quote after a discovery call. Custom builds start at $5,000 and go up to $20,000 depending on scope. Payment is by GCash, Maya, BPI or Metrobank bank transfer, or Wise, with a 50% deposit before work starts. You own the domain, site, and all content outright on full payment, with no lock-in contract.</p>
        <p>To see the full scope of what a web development engagement with us covers, visit <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/services#web">our web design and development services page</a>.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>Can a standard seven-day package include ecommerce?</strong> A standard fixed-price build covers a business website: pages, copy, a contact form, and basic SEO setup. Full ecommerce — a product catalogue, a payment gateway, cart and checkout — is custom development and is quoted separately after a brief. The timeline depends on the scope agreed before work starts.</p>
        <p><strong>Which payment gateway should I use for a Philippine online store?</strong> GCash and Maya are the most widely used consumer payment methods in the Philippines, and many developers integrate PayMongo or Xendit as the backend processor because they support multiple methods through a single API. The right gateway depends on your customers, your transaction volume, and the settlement terms that work for your cash flow.</p>
        <p><strong>Do I need a developer to manage my store after launch?</strong> For a platform-based store on WooCommerce or PrestaShop, adding products, updating prices, and publishing content is something you can do yourself. Security patches, plugin updates, and gateway configuration changes are where a care plan or ongoing developer support pays for itself.</p>
