        <p>A free website audit is a structured review of your site's speed, SEO basics, mobile experience and conversion signals — and a 15-minute check is enough to find the most common problems. Most small business sites have at least three fixable issues that affect how Google ranks them and how many visitors actually get in touch. Here is what a free website audit typically covers and what to do with the findings.</p>
        <h2>What a free website audit checks</h2>
        <p>An audit covers the things that affect both Google rankings and whether a visitor stays long enough to contact you. A 15-minute review typically checks speed first — how quickly the main page loads, whether the browser paints something useful above the fold, and whether the page jumps around as images and fonts arrive. Slow, unstable pages cause visitors to leave before reading, and Google's own guidance ties page experience directly to rankings.</p>
        <p>After speed, the check moves to mobile. Google indexes the mobile version of your site first, so a layout that only works on a widescreen is a problem even for desktop users. The review looks at whether text is readable without zooming, buttons are large enough to tap, and nothing overflows the screen on a phone.</p>
        <p>The third area is on-page SEO: whether each page has a clear title tag, a meta description, a single H1 heading, and location signals if you serve a specific town or region. These are not advanced tactics; they are the baseline that Google needs to understand what a page is about and who should see it. A site missing them leaves rankings on the table.</p>
        <p>The final area is conversion: whether a visitor can immediately see what you do, who you help, and how to contact you. A technically sound site that buries the contact details or explains the business in vague terms still fails to win enquiries.</p>
        <h2>What an audit usually finds</h2>
        <p>Most small business sites have the same handful of problems. Images are not compressed — a single large hero image can add several seconds of load time on a mobile connection. Page titles are either missing, duplicated across pages, or too long for Google to display in full. There is no sitemap submitted to Google Search Console, or the sitemap exists but links to pages blocked from indexing. The contact page has a phone number but no clear email address, or a business location is never mentioned on the site at all.</p>
        <p>These are not signs of a poorly built site. They are the natural result of a site built quickly without a final SEO pass, or a site that has grown by adding pages without reviewing the whole. A 15-minute check catches them before they cost you months of invisible rankings.</p>
        <h2>What to do with audit findings</h2>
        <p>Audit findings fall into two groups: things you can fix today and things that need a developer. Updating a page title, adding a meta description, or adding your address to the footer are all tasks a non-technical owner can handle in an afternoon. Compressing images, submitting a sitemap, or fixing a crawl error in your robots.txt file typically require a small amount of development time — but none of these is a major project.</p>
        <p>The audit itself is the starting point, not the finish line. A list of issues without a plan for fixing them is not useful. For a deeper look at the technical issues that most commonly affect rankings, our <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/blog/technical-seo-small-business">technical SEO guide for small businesses</a> covers sitemaps, crawlability, and the specific fixes that move rankings most.</p>
