      slug: 'get-more-google-reviews',
      title: "How to Get More Google Reviews: A Practical Guide for Small Business Owners",
      category: 'Strategy',
      date: '18 Oct 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Reancirl Balaba',
      authorRole: 'Co-Founder',
      authorPhoto: '',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1552664730-d307ca884978?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Google reviews influence how customers choose businesses — and how Google ranks them. Here's a practical guide to getting more of them consistently.",
      content: `
        <p>Google reviews are one of the most visible trust signals your business has. They appear directly in search results, on Google Maps, and in the knowledge panel that shows up when someone searches your business name. For a potential customer deciding between two similar businesses, five recent positive reviews can be the factor that determines who gets the call. The challenge is not collecting one or two reviews — it is building a steady, ongoing stream of them.</p>
        <h2>Why Google reviews affect more than just perception</h2>
        <p>Reviews on Google influence two things simultaneously: how potential customers perceive your business, and how Google ranks it in local search results. Google's local ranking algorithm considers the quantity, recency, and average rating of reviews as signals of legitimacy and relevance. A business with 40 reviews and a 4.5-star average will generally rank higher in local search than a competitor with 8 reviews and a 4.9-star average, because quantity and recency carry significant weight alongside rating.</p>
        <p><strong>This means a business that stops receiving new reviews gradually loses ground even if its existing reviews are positive.</strong> A cluster of reviews from two years ago tells Google less about your current standing than a consistent stream of recent ones. Building reviews is not a one-off task — it is an ongoing practice.</p>
        <h2>The simplest way to get reviews: just ask</h2>
        <p>The most effective method for collecting Google reviews is direct, personal requests. Most customers who have had a positive experience are willing to leave a review if asked — the barrier is not unwillingness, it is friction. Asking a customer to "leave a Google review" sends them to search for your business themselves, find the right listing, and navigate to the review panel. Most people will not complete that process.</p>
        <p>The solution is to remove as much of that friction as possible. Generate a direct link to your Google review form from your Google Business Profile and share it directly with customers by text message, email, or messaging app. The link takes them straight to the form with a single click. <strong>A direct link and a timely request — sent immediately after a positive interaction — will produce far more reviews than a vague instruction to leave feedback.</strong></p>
        <h2>Build a follow-up sequence into your workflow</h2>
        <p>Asking for reviews once, inconsistently, produces inconsistent results. Businesses that collect reviews steadily treat the request as part of their standard workflow rather than something they remember to do occasionally. The timing of the request matters: a customer is most likely to leave a review in the first 24 to 48 hours after a completed service, while the experience is still fresh.</p>
        <p>For service businesses, a short follow-up message the day after project completion works well. For retail, a receipt or confirmation email with a review link included at the bottom costs nothing to add and reaches every customer automatically. For businesses that handle a smaller number of larger clients, a personal message from the project owner or account manager carries more weight than an automated email and typically generates a higher response rate. Whatever your workflow, the goal is to make the review request automatic rather than manual so it happens with every client, not just the ones you remember.</p>
        <h2>How to respond to reviews — positive and negative</h2>
        <p>Responding to reviews is part of building a strong Google presence. Google rewards active engagement with a business listing, and potential customers read responses as carefully as they read the reviews themselves. A business that thanks reviewers by name, mentions the specific service, and signs off with a genuine note signals that real people run the operation and care about their customers' experience.</p>
        <p>Negative reviews require a measured response. Responding publicly with a professional, non-defensive acknowledgment — offering to discuss the issue directly and resolve it — tells every reader who sees the exchange more about your professionalism than the negative review does. <strong>A complaint handled visibly and well can be a stronger trust signal than an unbroken stream of five-star reviews, because it shows how you behave when something goes wrong.</strong> Never dispute or dismiss a negative review in public; it consistently backfires.</p>
        <h2>What not to do</h2>
        <p>Google prohibits incentivised reviews — offering discounts, gifts, or any form of compensation in exchange for a review. Beyond the policy violation, incentivised reviews tend to read as inauthentic and often lack the specific detail that makes a review persuasive. Bulk review services that use fake accounts are a more serious risk: Google detects and removes them, and businesses caught using them can have their listing suspended. The short-term boost is not worth the exposure.</p>
        <p>Equally, never ask customers to leave reviews from inside your office or on a device you provide. Google filters reviews submitted from the same IP address or device in close succession. Reviews collected this way are often removed before they ever appear.</p>
        <h2>A sustainable review strategy</h2>
        <p>The businesses with the strongest Google review profiles did not get there with a campaign — they got there by making the request a consistent part of how they operate. Set up a direct review link. Ask every customer at the right moment. Follow up within 24 hours of a completed service. Respond to every review you receive. These four steps, applied consistently, compound over months into a review profile that builds trust with potential customers and strengthens your position in local search. <strong>For small businesses competing in local markets, reviews are one of the highest-return activities available — they cost nothing to collect and continue working for you long after the customer has moved on.</strong> If you want help making your Google Business Profile work harder alongside your website, the team at <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com">Fazed Digital</a> combines local SEO with web design to give your business a stronger online presence.</p>
      `,
