    {
      slug: 'website-navigation-design',
      title: "Website Navigation Design: How to Structure Your Menu So Visitors Never Get Lost",
      category: 'Design',
      date: '11 Oct 2026',
      readTime: '4 min read',
      author: 'Lord Arcamo',
      authorRole: 'Founder',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-lord.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1498050108023-c5249f4df085?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Confusing navigation is one of the top reasons visitors leave without converting. Here's how to design a clear, intuitive menu structure that keeps people moving toward your goal.",
      content: `
        <p>Navigation is the invisible architecture of your website. Most visitors never think about it — until it fails them. The moment someone lands on your site and cannot figure out how to find your services, your pricing, or your contact page, they leave. Not briefly confused — gone, probably to a competitor whose site made the path obvious. Yet navigation is consistently one of the last things small business owners think about when building a website, treated as a layout decision rather than a business one. It is not. It is the decision that shapes every conversion your website is capable of making.</p>
        <h2>Why navigation is a business decision, not a design one</h2>
        <p>Most small business owners think of their navigation menu as a directory — a list of pages on the website. It is actually a decision tool. Every label in your menu is a choice made on behalf of your visitor: about what matters, what is findable, and what action you want them to take next. A menu that lists "About," "Services," "Portfolio," "Blog," "FAQ," "Team," "Careers," and "Contact" is not helpful — it is analysis paralysis spread across a thin strip at the top of the page. A visitor who does not immediately see the path to what they need will not explore methodically. They will leave.</p>
        <h2>Five items is the right number for most small business menus</h2>
        <p>Research on working memory suggests people can comfortably hold around seven items in mind at once — but for website navigation, the practical limit is lower. <strong>Five top-level menu items is the sweet spot for most small business websites.</strong> The goal is not to list every page you have; it is to surface the five decisions a new visitor needs to make, in the order they will naturally make them.</p>
        <p>For a service business, those five are typically: what the business offers, why this business in particular, evidence it has done good work, what working with them involves or costs, and how to start. That maps cleanly to: Services, About, Work or Case Studies, Process or Pricing, and Contact. Everything else — blog posts, team bios, FAQs — lives one level deeper, accessible but not competing for attention on the primary navigation bar.</p>
        <h2>Label your menu items in your customer's language</h2>
        <p>The labels you choose matter as much as the structure itself. "Solutions," "Offerings," or "What We Do" are labels that serve the business's internal vocabulary, not the visitor's mental model. Your customers search for "web design services" or "branding packages" — not "creative solutions." Use the words your customers actually use when describing what they need, and your navigation immediately becomes more intuitive.</p>
        <p>This principle applies to your primary call to action in the navigation too. <strong>"Book a Free Call" consistently converts better than "Contact"</strong> because it names an action with momentum rather than a generic destination. A visitor who reads "Contact" imagines a form. A visitor who reads "Book a Free Call" imagines a conversation. The framing changes the perceived effort of clicking — and that gap has a real effect on how many visitors follow through.</p>
        <h2>Mobile navigation: the version most small businesses are not testing</h2>
        <p>More than 60 percent of web traffic arrives on mobile devices, and mobile navigation behaves completely differently from desktop. The standard hamburger icon — three horizontal lines that open a menu on tap — is widely understood, but only works if executed correctly. The icon must be large enough to tap without precision: 44 by 44 pixels is the minimum recommended tap target. The menu panel that opens should be full-screen or near full-screen, with items spaced generously so adjacent labels are not tapped by accident.</p>
        <p>The most common failure is hiding the primary call to action inside the hamburger menu. On desktop, your "Book a Call" button sits visibly in the top-right corner at all times. On mobile, if it disappears into the menu, most visitors will never reach it. <strong>Test your mobile navigation by using your own website one-handed on a phone, without zooming.</strong> If you can reach your contact page in under three taps, your mobile navigation is working.</p>
        <h2>Sticky navigation and when it actually helps</h2>
        <p>A sticky navigation bar — one that stays fixed at the top of the screen as the visitor scrolls — is the right choice for most small business websites. As someone reads down a long service page, the ability to act at any moment without scrolling back to the top removes real friction from the conversion path. Every time they feel ready to reach out, the button is immediately visible.</p>
        <p>The exception is highly visual or narrative pages where a persistent bar visually competes with the content. In those cases, section-level CTAs embedded in the page body serve the same purpose without the constant visual weight. Evaluate based on page length and layout: a detailed services page with multiple sections benefits strongly from sticky navigation; a short, punchy landing page with a single clear call to action typically does not need it.</p>
        <h2>Test your navigation with real people before launch</h2>
        <p>Navigation assumptions are best confirmed with real people who have never seen your site. Show five people — not colleagues or family members who already know your business — and ask them to find your services, your pricing, and your contact page. Watch without guiding them. Every point of hesitation is a label, structure, or hierarchy issue you can fix before it costs you real visitors.</p>
        <p>The five-second test is equally useful: show someone your homepage for five seconds, then ask what the site does and what they should do next. If they cannot answer both questions correctly, your navigation and hero section are not working together. The fix is almost always simpler labeling and a more visible primary call to action — not a full redesign. Good navigation is invisible: when it works, visitors never notice it. They simply find what they need and take the action you want. Every website we build through our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> is structured around a clear, tested navigation framework designed to move visitors toward a conversion from the first scroll.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'schema-markup-small-business',
      title: "Schema Markup for Small Businesses: How Structured Data Gets You More Clicks on Google",
      category: 'SEO',
      date: '10 Oct 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Russel Heyrana',
      authorRole: 'Marketing Director',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-russel.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1460925895917-afdab827c52f?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Most websites rank without rich results and lose clicks to lower-ranked competitors. Here's how schema markup works and which types actually move the needle for small businesses.",
      content: `
        <p>Most small business websites show up in Google as a plain blue link with a short description. Some show up with star ratings, FAQ dropdowns, or review counts directly in the search results. That second version earns significantly more clicks — an average of 20 to 30 percent more than a plain result, even when it ranks lower. The difference is schema markup: a small amount of structured data added to your website that tells Google exactly what your page contains. It does not replace solid SEO fundamentals. It amplifies them. And for small businesses, the types of schema that deliver the most impact are surprisingly straightforward to implement.</p>
        <h2>What schema markup actually is</h2>
        <p>Schema markup is code added to your website's HTML — in a format called JSON-LD — that labels your content for search engines. It is not visible to visitors; it is read by Google's crawlers. Think of it as a translation layer: instead of Google having to infer what "Mon–Fri 8:30–5:00" means, a LocalBusiness schema explicitly tells it these are your operating hours. Instead of guessing that the stars on your page are customer reviews, a Review schema confirms it. Google uses that precision to build rich results — the enhanced search listings that include stars, FAQs, prices, or event dates — which stand out visually against plain results and consistently earn higher click-through rates from the same ranking position.</p>
        <h2>The five schema types that matter most for small businesses</h2>
        <p>Not all schema types deliver real return for the effort. These five do.</p>
        <p><strong>LocalBusiness</strong> schema is the highest priority for any business serving a specific area. It specifies your exact name, address, phone number, opening hours, and service area — the same signals Google uses for Map Pack ranking. Combining LocalBusiness schema with an optimised Google Business Profile strengthens your local authority across both organic results and maps.</p>
        <p><strong>FAQ</strong> schema lets you mark up a question-and-answer section on your page. When Google picks it up, your search result expands to show two or three accordion-style FAQs directly beneath your listing — occupying significantly more screen space and addressing objections before the visitor clicks. A services page with three well-chosen questions (Is this available in your city? How long does it take? What does it cost?) can substantially improve qualified click-through rates.</p>
        <p><strong>Review</strong> schema marks up the aggregate ratings from your page — your star score and review count — so they appear as stars directly in the search result. A result showing five stars and 47 reviews in Google attracts clicks that plain results simply do not.</p>
        <p><strong>BreadcrumbList</strong> schema tells Google the hierarchy of your page within your site. This shows the URL as a navigational path in the result — "yoursite.com › services › web-design" — which communicates site structure and reassures visitors that they will land in the right place.</p>
        <p><strong>Article</strong> schema is valuable for blog posts. It confirms the author, publication date, and content type, which supports inclusion in Google's Top Stories carousel and article rich results for relevant queries. If you publish regular content, this schema type compounds over time.</p>
        <h2>How to add schema markup to your website</h2>
        <p>The recommended method is JSON-LD: a script block added to the head section of your page HTML. It sits alongside your content invisibly and defines your business properties in a structured format Google reads reliably. You do not need to modify your visible page content at all.</p>
        <p>On WordPress, the free Rank Math and Yoast SEO plugins generate LocalBusiness, Article, and FAQ schema automatically from your page content and plugin settings — no code required for most types. On a custom-built site, the JSON-LD block goes directly into the page template head. Google's own Structured Data Markup Helper lets you highlight elements on your live page and auto-generate schema code from them — a practical zero-code starting point for business owners without a developer on hand.</p>
        <p><strong>One important constraint:</strong> only mark up content that genuinely appears on the page. Schema that describes content the page does not contain is a Google policy violation and can result in a manual penalty that removes your rich results entirely. If your FAQ schema lists questions not actually shown on the page, remove it before it goes live.</p>
        <h2>Testing your schema before it goes live</h2>
        <p>Google provides two free tools to validate your markup. The <strong>Rich Results Test</strong> (search.google.com/test/rich-results) takes a URL or a code snippet and tells you which rich result types your page qualifies for and whether any errors prevent them from displaying. The <strong>Schema Markup Validator</strong> (validator.schema.org) checks your code structure against the full schema.org specification and surfaces property-level errors.</p>
        <p>Test both tools after any schema implementation. Common errors include missing required properties — LocalBusiness requires at minimum @type, name, address, and telephone — incorrectly formatted phone numbers (Google expects international format: +639068253451), and mismatched @type values. A schema block with errors earns no rich result at all, so fixing errors before the next Google crawl is essential.</p>
        <h2>What to expect — and how long it takes</h2>
        <p>Adding schema markup does not change your ranking position. It changes what your result looks like when it ranks. The typical timeline: Google re-crawls your updated pages within a few days to two weeks. Eligibility for rich results then shows in Google Search Console under the Enhancements tab — look for FAQ or Review result types appearing as new report entries. Most businesses see the enhanced display within four to six weeks of a correct, error-free implementation.</p>
        <p>The impact on click-through rate can be significant. <strong>A page that gained FAQ rich results at rank four often generates as many clicks as the same page would at rank two without them.</strong> Schema is not a shortcut around building domain authority — it is the layer that makes your existing SEO investment work harder in the listings where the decision to click or keep scrolling is made in under two seconds.</p>
        <p>Start with LocalBusiness schema on your homepage and primary service pages, then add FAQ schema to the pages where you already answer customer questions. Run the Rich Results Test on each one. Fix any errors. Then check your Search Console Enhancements reports monthly to confirm the rich results are appearing. It is one of the highest return-on-time SEO investments a small business can make. Every website we build through our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> ships with LocalBusiness, BreadcrumbList, and Article schema correctly implemented from day one — so structured data works in your favour from the moment you launch.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'brand-voice-guide',
      title: "Brand Voice for Small Businesses: How to Define How Your Business Communicates",
      category: 'Branding',
      date: '09 Oct 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Lord Arcamo',
      authorRole: 'Founder',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-lord.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1542744173-8e7e53415bb0?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Your business already has a voice — but is it the one you chose? Here's how to define your brand voice and make sure every word you write sounds deliberate.",
      content: `
        <p>Every business has a tone of voice — most just have not chosen it deliberately. When your website sounds different from your social media, which sounds different from your staff on the phone, visitors feel the inconsistency even if they cannot name it. That disconnect costs you trust, and trust is what converts strangers into customers. Defining your brand voice is not a creative exercise for big brands. It is a practical decision that affects every word your business puts in front of a customer.</p>
        <h2>What brand voice is — and what it is not</h2>
        <p>Brand voice is not your logo, your colour palette, or your tagline. It is the personality that comes through in every word your business writes and speaks. It is the difference between "We would love to help you find the perfect solution" and "Tell us what you need — we will sort it." Both are friendly. Neither is wrong. But they project very different personalities, and your customers will form an impression from that difference whether you planned it or not.</p>
        <p>Voice and tone are related but distinct. <strong>Voice is consistent</strong> — it is the character of your brand, the same across every context. <strong>Tone adapts</strong> — more formal in a contract, lighter in a social caption, reassuring in a refund email. Getting voice right first makes tone variations feel natural rather than inconsistent.</p>
        <h2>Why voice consistency matters more than most businesses realise</h2>
        <p>Consistency builds familiarity, and familiarity builds trust. When a prospect reads your website, then sees your Facebook post, then receives your proposal and all three sound like the same person wrote them, they feel like they already know you. That familiarity reduces the mental friction of deciding to hire or buy.</p>
        <p>The opposite effect is equally real. A polished website followed by a casual, typo-laden WhatsApp reply makes a prospect wonder which version is the real business. You earned their trust with the website; the inconsistency spends it immediately. <strong>Larger brands invest heavily in voice guidelines precisely because the compounding effect of consistent communication — across years, channels, and staff — is one of the most durable competitive advantages a business can build.</strong> Small businesses can build the same advantage with a fraction of the effort, because they typically have fewer people to align.</p>
        <h2>Three questions that define your voice</h2>
        <p>The fastest way to define your brand voice is to answer three questions honestly. <strong>Who are you to your customer?</strong> Are you an expert they hire for your knowledge, a partner who works alongside them, or a trusted local business they can call any time? Each relationship implies a different voice — authoritative, collaborative, or approachable. <strong>What do you want customers to feel after interacting with your content?</strong> Reassured, energised, confident, or understood? Your word choices, sentence length, and use of humour all dial different emotions up or down.</p>
        <p><strong>What three adjectives describe your brand if it were a person?</strong> Write down ten, then cut to the three most honest — not the three most aspirational, the three most accurate. Those three become your filter for every piece of copy: does this sound direct, local, and warm? If not, rewrite it. A useful shortcut is to find two or three brands outside your industry whose communication style you admire and analyse specifically what you like about it. That analysis often surfaces what you are trying to build faster than starting from scratch.</p>
        <h2>Translating voice into actual copy</h2>
        <p>Voice defined on paper means nothing until it changes what you write. Take one page of your website and rewrite a paragraph through the lens of your three adjectives. If your brand is direct, local, and warm, compare these two versions of the same message.</p>
        <p>Version one: "Our professional team of specialists is committed to delivering comprehensive digital solutions tailored to the unique requirements of each client." Version two: "We are a small team from Iligan City. We build websites that bring in real customers — no retainer for services you do not need." Same facts. Completely different personality. The second version communicates more about the business in fewer words, and that personality is something a reader can respond to.</p>
        <p>Do this exercise for your homepage headline, your About page, and your contact form confirmation message. These three touchpoints cover the beginning, middle, and end of a first impression — and together they set the expectation for every interaction that follows.</p>
        <h2>How to document it so your whole team sounds the same</h2>
        <p>A brand voice is only useful if everyone who communicates on your behalf uses it — staff, contractors, a social media manager, anyone who sends an email or writes a caption under your name. That requires a short written reference: not a 40-page brand guidelines PDF, but a one-page voice guide that anyone can read in five minutes.</p>
        <p>A useful voice guide has five parts: your three voice adjectives with a short description of each; two to three "we sound like this / we do not sound like this" examples per adjective; a vocabulary list of words you use and words you avoid; the emotional state you want your customer in after reading your content; and one strong example of your voice done well — an email, a social post, or a web paragraph. Build it into your onboarding for anyone who touches your communications. Share it with any agency or freelancer creating content in your name. Review it when your business changes significantly.</p>
        <p>The hour you invest in creating this document will save dozens of hours of back-and-forth on whether something "sounds right", and it will compound in the consistency of every customer touchpoint from this point forward. Every website we build through our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> is written with your brand voice as the brief — not generic placeholder copy that you have to rewrite later.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'compete-with-larger-competitors',
      title: "How Small Businesses Can Compete With Larger Competitors (And Win)",
      category: 'Strategy',
      date: '08 Oct 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Reancirl Balaba',
      authorRole: 'Co-Founder',
      authorPhoto: 'https://fazeddigital.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/117749302_4540601115965165_6039017566881807039_n-450x450.jpg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1542744173-8e7e53415bb0?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Big companies have budget and brand recognition. Here's how smart small businesses use speed, specificity, and relationships to win in markets dominated by larger players.",
      content: `
        <p>The most common complaint we hear from small business owners is some version of this: "How do we compete against companies with bigger budgets, larger teams, and more established names?" It is the wrong question. The right question is: what advantages do you have that they do not — and how do you build your strategy around those advantages instead of fighting on their terms? Large companies have scale. You have speed, specificity, and the ability to genuinely know your customers. Those are not consolation prizes. Used correctly, they are decisive competitive advantages.</p>
        <h2>Stop competing on price — compete on specificity</h2>
        <p>The first instinct for a small business competing against a larger player is to undercut on price. This is almost always a losing strategy. Large companies can sustain a price war far longer than you can, and commodity pricing erodes your margins while positioning your brand as the cheap option rather than the smart choice.</p>
        <p>The more effective move is to become the obvious specialist. A large digital marketing agency serves clients across every industry. A small agency that focuses exclusively on e-commerce brands in the Philippines, or on healthcare providers, or on food businesses, knows that vertical deeply — the specific compliance issues, the seasonal patterns, the language that resonates with customers. <strong>Specificity is a competitive moat that scale cannot easily replicate.</strong> When a business in your target niche searches for a provider, they will nearly always prefer the specialist over the generalist, and they will pay more for it.</p>
        <h2>Use your size as a speed advantage</h2>
        <p>Large organisations move slowly. A decision that takes your business a day can take a corporation three weeks — through department approvals, vendor processes, committee reviews, and sign-off chains. That speed gap is a real advantage in every market where responsiveness matters.</p>
        <p>When a client needs something changed quickly, say yes and do it fast. When an opportunity opens in your market — a competitor's pricing shift, a new platform, a change in customer behaviour — move on it immediately. Large companies often know about the same shifts months before they can act on them. You can act today. <strong>Being known as the business that responds fast and delivers without drama is a sustainable differentiator.</strong> Clients who have been frustrated by slow turnaround times and rotating account managers at larger firms will pay a premium for it — and they will tell others.</p>
        <h2>Build the relationships large companies have outsourced</h2>
        <p>Large companies are very good at systems. They are much worse at personal relationships. Their clients are accounts in a CRM, managed by staff who may change every twelve months. Your clients know your name, your phone number, and the story of what you built together.</p>
        <p>That relationship density is enormously valuable. A client who trusts you personally refers you constantly — not because of a referral programme, but because recommending you costs them nothing and reflects well on them. A client who feels like an account number at a large firm has no particular motivation to refer you to anyone. Invest deliberately in your client relationships. Remember details. Follow up after projects close. Celebrate their wins publicly. The compound return on those investments — in referrals, renewals, and the resistance clients show to competitors who come knocking — is one of the clearest advantages a small business has over a large one.</p>
        <h2>Own your local market before going broad</h2>
        <p>One of the most consistent strategic errors small businesses make is trying to compete nationally or globally before they have dominated their own backyard. A web design agency in Iligan City that is the best-known, most-recommended option in Iligan City has a sustainable, defensible business. The same agency competing for the same keyword space as Manila firms is invisible.</p>
        <p>Local search, local media, local business networks, local word of mouth — these are arenas where a small business can genuinely dominate, and where a large company has no real presence. <strong>Own your local market completely before you scale.</strong> Local dominance is far more profitable and far more sustainable than national mediocrity. Once you have a reputation and a referral engine locally, the pathway to broader markets opens naturally — on your terms, at your pace.</p>
        <h2>Your online presence is the great equaliser</h2>
        <p>A well-built website with strong SEO, a clear value proposition, and genuine client testimonials competes directly with any competitor's online presence, regardless of their budget. Google does not serve results based on company size — it serves results based on relevance, technical quality, and authority. A small business that has invested properly in its site, its content, and its Google Business Profile will consistently outrank a large competitor that has neglected theirs.</p>
        <p>This is the one area where a small business can appear — and genuinely be — exactly as credible as a much larger competitor to a prospect conducting research. Someone who finds you through a well-ranked blog post or a polished local map listing has no idea whether you are a two-person team or a two-hundred-person agency. What they see is the quality of your content, the clarity of your positioning, and the credibility of your social proof. All of those are within your control regardless of your budget.</p>
        <p>Competing with larger businesses is not about matching their resources. It is about building a strategy that wins on the dimensions they are structurally weak on: speed, specificity, relationships, local authority, and a digital presence that reflects real expertise. Our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> is built around exactly this premise — giving small businesses the online credibility and performance to compete and win in their market from day one.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'website-accessibility-guide',
      title: "Website Accessibility for Small Businesses: What It Is, Why It Matters, and How to Start",
      category: 'Development',
      date: '07 Oct 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Justin Sumaya',
      authorRole: 'Head of Development',
      authorPhoto: 'https://fazeddigital.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/151652330_3908937289163068_8338977159966182887_n-450x450.jpg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1498050108023-c5249f4df085?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Inaccessible websites lock out 15% of potential customers and carry growing legal risk. Here's a practical accessibility guide for small business owners.",
      content: `
        <p>Accessibility is consistently the last thing on a small business owner's web design brief, usually because it is assumed to be a concern for large organisations or government agencies. That assumption is wrong, and it has a cost. Approximately 15% of the world's population lives with some form of disability — visual, hearing, motor or cognitive. When a website is inaccessible, that is not a technical abstraction: it is a real group of potential customers who cannot read your services, fill in your contact form, or find your phone number. Fixing that is not as complicated as it sounds. Most small business websites can address their most significant accessibility failures with a handful of targeted, low-cost improvements.</p>
        <h2>What web accessibility actually means</h2>
        <p>It is not only screen readers and blind users. Web accessibility covers a wide spectrum: users with low vision who increase browser text size, users with motor impairments who navigate with a keyboard instead of a mouse, users with cognitive disabilities who rely on clear and consistent layouts, and users who are temporarily impaired — a broken arm, bright sunlight on a phone screen, or a noisy environment where they cannot listen to audio. The governing technical standard is the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG), currently at version 2.1. <strong>WCAG 2.1 Level AA is the practical target for most businesses</strong> — it is the level referenced by most legal frameworks and the level that developers, browsers, and assistive technology universally understand.</p>
        <h2>The business case: beyond doing the right thing</h2>
        <p>Serving 15% of potential customers better is reason enough. But accessible websites also rank better on Google. Alt text on images, clear heading structure, keyboard-navigable menus and fast load times are all signals Google uses to evaluate page quality. Many WCAG fixes overlap directly with core SEO fundamentals: descriptive links, logical page structure, legible text, and proper semantic HTML all help both search engines and assistive technology.</p>
        <p>Accessibility improvements also benefit all users, not just those with disabilities. Subtitles help in loud environments. High-contrast text is easier to read in sunlight. Clear layouts are faster to scan for everyone. The legal risk is also growing: accessibility lawsuits and regulatory requirements are increasing across the US, Europe, and Australia, and the trend is not reversing.</p>
        <h2>The five most common failures on small business websites</h2>
        <p>Most accessibility audits on small business sites surface the same problems repeatedly. Fix these five and you will resolve the majority of real barriers.</p>
        <p><strong>Missing alt text on images.</strong> Every meaningful image on your site needs an alt attribute describing its content. Decorative images should carry alt="" so screen readers skip them. An image of your product with no alt text is invisible to a visually impaired user — and to Google.</p>
        <p><strong>Insufficient colour contrast.</strong> WCAG requires a contrast ratio of at least 4.5:1 between body text and its background. Many small business websites use light grey text on white — visually subtle, but failing the standard and hard to read for anyone with reduced vision. Test your text contrast with a free contrast checker before your site launches.</p>
        <p><strong>Unlabelled form fields.</strong> A form input associated with a label only by visual proximity — not by HTML markup — has no accessible label for screen readers. Use proper label elements linked to their corresponding input fields so assistive technology can describe what each field is for.</p>
        <p><strong>Vague link text.</strong> Links that read "click here" or "learn more" give no context to a user navigating by screen reader, which typically lists all links on a page independently of surrounding text. "Download the pricing guide" or "Book a free strategy call" describe the destination clearly and work in any context.</p>
        <p><strong>No keyboard navigation.</strong> Every interactive element — menus, buttons, forms, modals — should be reachable and usable with the Tab key alone, with a visible focus indicator showing where the keyboard is. Try navigating your website without a mouse. If you cannot complete your contact form using only Tab, Enter and arrow keys, your site has a real barrier affecting a significant number of users.</p>
        <h2>Free tools to find your issues right now</h2>
        <p>You do not need specialist software to start. The <strong>WAVE Web Accessibility Evaluator</strong> is a free browser extension that overlays errors and warnings directly on your own site within seconds. <strong>Google Lighthouse</strong>, built into Chrome DevTools under the Audits tab, includes an accessibility score alongside its performance audit and gives specific, actionable recommendations. <strong>axe DevTools</strong> has a free browser extension version that surfaces WCAG 2.1 failures with clear explanations and links to fixes. And the keyboard test above costs nothing and reveals real issues immediately — no tools required.</p>
        <h2>How to start without feeling overwhelmed</h2>
        <p>Accessibility is a spectrum, not a binary. Do not wait until you can address every WCAG criterion before starting. <strong>Fix colour contrast and missing alt text first.</strong> These two issues alone account for the majority of WCAG failures on most small business sites, and both are quick to resolve without touching your site's code structure. Then address form labels and link text. Then test keyboard navigation and fix what is broken. That sequence handles the most critical barriers without requiring a full rebuild.</p>
        <p>Build it into your process going forward. When a developer adds an image, they add alt text. When a designer picks a colour combination, they check the contrast ratio. Accessibility fails silently when it is treated as a periodic audit exercise rather than a standard practice on every new piece of work.</p>
        <p>An accessible website is a better website — clearer to read, easier to navigate, faster to rank, and open to more potential customers. Every improvement you make extends your reach. Every site we build through our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> ships with semantic HTML, proper alt text, accessible colour contrast, and keyboard-navigable components as standard — not as an add-on.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'link-building-small-business',
      title: "Link Building for Small Businesses: How to Earn Backlinks That Actually Improve Your Rankings",
      category: 'SEO',
      date: '06 Oct 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Russel Heyrana',
      authorRole: 'Marketing Director',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-russel.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1460925895917-afdab827c52f?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Backlinks remain one of Google's strongest ranking signals, yet most small businesses ignore them. Here's a practical guide to earning your first real links.",
      content: `
        <p>Ask any small business owner if they have done link building and most will say no — either because they do not know what it is, or because they have read enough about it to feel overwhelmed. That is a costly gap to leave open. Backlinks — links from other websites pointing to yours — remain one of the most heavily weighted signals in Google's ranking algorithm. A site with consistent, genuine backlinks will almost always outrank one without them, regardless of how well everything else is optimised.</p>
        <h2>Why backlinks matter more than most small businesses realise</h2>
        <p>Google originally built its algorithm around the premise that a website other people link to must be trustworthy and relevant. That logic still holds. When an authoritative website links to yours, it passes some of its trust to you — a signal Google calls PageRank. The more credible the linking site, the more weight the link carries.</p>
        <p>For new and growing sites, this matters enormously. Two websites with identical on-page optimisation will rank very differently if one has 30 backlinks from relevant sources and the other has none. <strong>On-page SEO gets you in the game. Backlinks determine where you finish.</strong></p>
        <h2>The backlinks worth building — and the ones to ignore</h2>
        <p>Not all links are equal. A backlink from a relevant industry directory or a local news article is worth far more than a hundred links from unrelated, low-quality sites. Google has become very good at identifying link schemes, and a site flagged for manipulative linking can be penalised — dropping from rankings it took months to earn.</p>
        <p>Focus on three qualities: <strong>relevance</strong> (links from sites in your industry or geographic area), <strong>authority</strong> (links from sites Google already trusts — established publications, government portals, universities, and industry associations), and <strong>editorial placement</strong> (links that appear naturally in content because the author genuinely references your site, not because you paid for them). A single editorial link from a respected local business publication is worth more than fifty links from generic directories.</p>
        <h2>Your first 10 backlinks: the ones you can get this week</h2>
        <p>Every business has access to a set of easy, legitimate backlinks they have not claimed yet. <strong>Business directories</strong>: ensure your business is listed on Google Business Profile, Yelp, Foursquare, Bing Places, and the Philippine DTI's business directory, and make sure your website URL is linked from each profile. These are not high-authority links, but they establish baseline citation signals and cost nothing.</p>
        <p><strong>Suppliers and partners</strong>: if you have suppliers, vendors, manufacturers or business partners with websites, ask whether they list their clients or stockists. A "where to buy" page or a client showcase is a natural link opportunity most businesses miss. <strong>Industry associations and chambers of commerce</strong>: most charge a membership fee, but the backlink from a trusted association site is worth it from both a referral and an SEO standpoint. The Iligan City Chamber of Commerce, the Philippine Chamber of Commerce, and any industry body relevant to your sector are strong starting points.</p>
        <h2>Content that earns links without asking</h2>
        <p>The most scalable link-building approach is creating content other people want to reference. This does not mean producing content constantly — it means producing specific types that earn links by being genuinely useful to others.</p>
        <p><strong>Local guides and explainers</strong> are especially effective for small businesses. A "Complete Guide to Business Permits in Iligan City" or a step-by-step process guide in your industry answers a question nobody else has answered well. Local journalists, bloggers and businesses will link to it when it is the best available resource on the topic. <strong>Original data</strong>, even on a small scale — a survey of 50 clients, a benchmark you have measured in your own operations — gives writers something to cite that they cannot get elsewhere. <strong>Free tools and templates</strong> work similarly: a pricing calculator, a downloadable checklist or a simple planning template earns links from every website that recommends it to its own readers.</p>
        <h2>Local link building: the most underused strategy in the Philippines</h2>
        <p>Local businesses in the Philippines have a link-building advantage that many international SEO guides overlook: an active local media and community ecosystem. Philippine cities and provinces have local news sites, community-run websites, and city or barangay government portals that sometimes link to registered local businesses and civic contributors.</p>
        <p>Reach out to local news sites about genuinely newsworthy things: a business milestone, a community initiative, or an unusual service that would interest local readers. Sponsoring a community event typically results in a link from the organiser's website and any press coverage the event receives. Writing a guest column for a local business publication — offering practical advice in your area of expertise — earns an author link and positions you as a credible voice. None of these strategies require a budget. They require initiative and follow-through.</p>
        <h2>What to avoid — and how to tell the difference</h2>
        <p>The line between legitimate link building and manipulative tactics is usually clear: paying for links on a site that has no editorial standards, buying backlinks in bulk from services promising volume, participating in link exchange rings, or creating fake blog networks to host your links. Google's manual review team actively investigates these patterns, and a manual penalty can remove a site from search results entirely — undoing months of legitimate work.</p>
        <p><strong>One test worth applying</strong>: would you be comfortable showing this link to Google? If it is a paid link disguised as editorial, or a directory that exists only to sell links, do not build it. The short-term gain is not worth the risk to a site you have spent months building.</p>
        <p>Link building is slow, which is why most businesses skip it. But compounded consistently it becomes the most defensible advantage in your market — a competitor can copy your on-page SEO overnight, but they cannot replicate years of genuine, earned authority. Every site launched through our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> ships with a clean technical foundation that maximises the ranking value of every legitimate link you earn.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'visual-hierarchy-web-design',
      title: "Visual Hierarchy in Web Design: How to Guide Visitors to the Action That Matters",
      category: 'Design',
      date: '05 Oct 2026',
      readTime: '4 min read',
      author: 'Lord Arcamo',
      authorRole: 'Founder',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-lord.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1524781289445-ddf8f5695861?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Most websites make visitors work to figure out where to look. Here's how to use size, colour and spacing to guide them straight to your call to action.",
      content: `
        <p>Every time a visitor lands on your website, their eye moves before their brain does. In a fraction of a second they scan the page and form a judgment about where to look, what matters most, and whether this site is worth their time. Visual hierarchy is the deliberate arrangement of elements that guides that instinct. When it is done well, visitors flow toward your most important message and your call to action without thinking about it. When it is absent, they get lost and leave.</p>
        <h2>What visual hierarchy actually is</h2>
        <p>Visual hierarchy is the principle that some elements on a page should visually shout while others whisper, and that the order of shouting should match the order of importance. It is not the same as good taste or attractive design — a beautiful page can have no hierarchy at all. The goal is direction: helping each visitor immediately understand what this page is about, what they should read next, and what they should do.</p>
        <p>Every page element — headline, image, body text, button, icon — either supports or undermines this direction. The tools you use are size, colour, contrast, spacing, and typography weight. Most small business websites apply these inconsistently or not at all, which is why visitors so often describe them as "confusing" or "overwhelming" without being able to say why.</p>
        <h2>Size and scale: the clearest signal</h2>
        <p>Size is the most direct hierarchy signal. Larger elements attract attention first. This is why your page headline should be significantly larger than your body copy, and why your call-to-action button should be large enough to catch the eye without dominating the surrounding content.</p>
        <p>A common mistake is designing everything at similar sizes to avoid looking pushy or over-designed. The result is a page with no clear starting point, where the visitor's eye has nowhere to anchor. Contrast in scale creates momentum. A headline at 48 pixels next to body copy at 16 pixels creates clear reading order. The same information presented in uniform text gives the visitor nothing to grip. <strong>If someone could fold your page into a thumbnail and still pick out the headline and button, your size hierarchy is working.</strong></p>
        <h2>Colour and contrast: guiding the eye instantly</h2>
        <p>Colour is the fastest hierarchy signal after size. A bright accent colour on a single call-to-action button in a mostly neutral layout draws the eye immediately. This is why consistent use of one accent colour, reserved almost exclusively for buttons and links, is such a reliable principle in web design.</p>
        <p>When accent colours appear too often — on headings, decorative elements, background blocks, and buttons alike — they lose their directing power. <strong>If everything is highlighted, nothing is.</strong> Reserve your strongest colour for the action you want people to take, and it will work every time. At Fazed Digital, our electric lime accent appears on exactly one element per section: the button. Everything else is neutral so that button never competes for attention.</p>
        <h2>Spacing and white space: separation is communication</h2>
        <p>White space — the empty areas around and between elements — is not wasted space. It is an active design tool that separates ideas, creates breathing room, and signals to the visitor that a new section or concept is beginning.</p>
        <p>Cramped pages feel overwhelming even when the content is good. Generous spacing around a headline makes it feel more important. Tight spacing between related items groups them as a unit. The most effective small business websites typically use far more white space than the client expects, and the restraint is almost always the right call. If your page feels busy, add space before changing anything else — it is usually the cheapest and most effective fix.</p>
        <h2>Typography weight and style: the quiet signal</h2>
        <p>Within body text, weight changes create local hierarchy. A sentence with one key phrase in bold stands out from the surrounding paragraph and catches a skimming reader. But like accent colours, this tool is effective only when used sparingly. A paragraph where half the text is bold loses the signal entirely — the visitor's eye stops treating it as emphasis and starts treating it as noise.</p>
        <p>Heading levels also carry hierarchy: an H2 above an H3 tells the visitor they are moving from a major topic to a subtopic. Many websites misuse heading tags purely for visual effect, using an H3 for something that should be a label or caption. That breaks both the reading experience and the SEO structure, since search engines use headings to understand what a page covers.</p>
        <h2>Applying hierarchy to your highest-stakes pages</h2>
        <p>Start with the pages that matter most: your homepage, your primary service page, and your contact or booking page. On each one, decide the single most important thing a visitor should notice first — almost always the headline — and make it clearly the largest element. Then identify the one action you want them to take — almost always a button — and give it the strongest colour contrast on the page. Everything else is supporting cast.</p>
        <p>A useful practical test: view your page blurred, or step back and squint at it. The elements that still stand out through the blur have the strongest hierarchy. If your blurred eye goes first to a decorative image rather than your headline or button, the hierarchy needs adjusting. Run this test on your homepage right now. Most businesses are surprised by what they find.</p>
        <p>Strong visual hierarchy is invisible to your visitors. They do not think about it — they just feel clear, guided and ready to act. Every website built through our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> is designed with visual hierarchy applied from the first screen down, so visitors always know exactly where to look and what to do next.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'logo-design-small-business',
      title: "Logo Design for Small Businesses: What Makes a Logo Actually Work",
      category: 'Branding',
      date: '04 Oct 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Lord Arcamo',
      authorRole: 'Founder',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-lord.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1552664730-d307ca884978?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Your logo is the most repeated visual in your business. Here's what makes one truly effective — and the design mistakes that quietly undermine your brand.",
      content: `
        <p>Your logo will appear on your website, your business card, your invoices, your social profiles, your signage, and anywhere else your business shows up. It is the most repeated visual in your marketing. Yet many small businesses treat it as an afterthought — choosing the cheapest option, rushing the process, or pulling something from a template site. A logo designed without intention costs more in the long run than one built properly from the start.</p>
        <h2>What your logo actually needs to do</h2>
        <p>A logo is not your entire brand — it is the entry point to it. Its job is to be instantly recognisable, communicate what kind of business you are, and hold up consistently across every surface it appears on. That last point — versatility — is the one most logos fail. A design that looks sharp on a website but turns into a muddy smudge on a business card or a blurry square on a social profile has not done its job.</p>
        <p>The best logos are simple enough to work at 16 pixels and bold enough to anchor a billboard. <strong>Complexity is the enemy of recognition.</strong> Every element in a logo that is not doing essential work is making the logo worse.</p>
        <h2>The five principles of effective logo design</h2>
        <p><strong>Simple and distinctive.</strong> A logo with too many elements is impossible to recognise quickly and difficult to reproduce consistently. The strongest brand marks — Nike, Apple, FedEx — are built on single, clean ideas. Simplicity is not laziness; it is discipline.</p>
        <p><strong>Versatile.</strong> Your logo needs to work in full colour, in single colour, in black, and in white — reversed on dark backgrounds, printed at tiny sizes, and embossed on merchandise. Test every version before committing. If any of them fail, the design needs adjusting.</p>
        <p><strong>Timeless over trendy.</strong> Gradient-heavy logos, 3D bevels, and font styles that feel cutting-edge today will look dated in three years. Lean toward clean, geometric forms and classic typography. Trends are for social posts. Your logo needs to last a decade.</p>
        <p><strong>Appropriate for your market.</strong> A playful, rounded typeface works for a children's brand and undermines trust for a law firm. Research your market, look at what your competitors are doing, and design within the right visual vocabulary — or break it deliberately, but with intention.</p>
        <p><strong>Legible at every size.</strong> Your logo text must be readable when the mark is small. If the business name disappears when shrunk down, the font or spacing needs work. Minimum safe size is a practical test every logo should pass before approval.</p>
        <h2>The files you actually need — and why</h2>
        <p>When a designer delivers a finished logo, you should receive at minimum four file types. <strong>SVG or EPS</strong> — vector formats that resize infinitely without losing quality, essential for printing and signage. <strong>PNG with a transparent background</strong> — for digital use on websites, social media, and slide decks where the logo needs to sit over colour or photography without a white box behind it. <strong>PNG on white background</strong> — for documents, email headers, invoices, and platforms that do not handle transparency. <strong>PDF</strong> — for sending to printers and commercial vendors.</p>
        <p>If a designer delivers only a JPG or a low-resolution PNG, the job is not finished. You are missing the files that will matter most when you actually need them — and scrambling for them later is expensive.</p>
        <h2>The logo design process — what good looks like</h2>
        <p>A professional logo process begins with discovery: understanding your business, your market, your competitors and the message you need to project. Brief first, design second. Designers who skip this and jump straight to showing you three random options are guessing, not designing.</p>
        <p>Good process moves from strategy through rough concepting — usually 10 to 20 quick sketches exploring different directions — then refines the strongest two or three to polished presentations, followed by revision rounds with clear, specific feedback. Expect at least two rounds before finalisation. Rushing the first concept through to approval because it looks fine on a white screen is how most logo regrets happen.</p>
        <h2>Common mistakes that cost small businesses</h2>
        <p><strong>Choosing based on personal preference alone.</strong> Your logo is not for you — it is for your customers. The question is not whether you like it; it is whether it will work for the people you are trying to attract.</p>
        <p><strong>Using too many fonts or colours.</strong> One primary typeface, one supporting typeface at most. A primary colour plus a neutral. Complexity makes inconsistency inevitable across applications, and inconsistency erodes brand recognition.</p>
        <p><strong>Designing before naming the business.</strong> The name and mark need to work together. A name change after a logo is locked means a logo change — and a wasted investment.</p>
        <p><strong>Choosing cheap and then redesigning a year later.</strong> A low-cost logo from a generic marketplace will typically lack the strategic thinking, file formats, and versatility you need. The total cost of a cheap logo followed by a proper rebrand almost always exceeds the cost of doing it right the first time. Your logo is a long-term asset — it deserves the same investment mindset as your other business infrastructure.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'google-analytics-4-small-business',
      title: "Google Analytics 4 for Small Businesses: What to Track and How to Set It Up",
      category: 'Development',
      date: '03 Oct 2026',
      readTime: '4 min read',
      author: 'Justin Sumaya',
      authorRole: 'Head of Development',
      authorPhoto: 'https://fazeddigital.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/151652330_3908937289163068_8338977159966182887_n-450x450.jpg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1461749280684-dccba630e2f6?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Most small businesses install Google Analytics and never look at it again. Here's how to set up GA4 properly and track the numbers that actually matter.",
      content: `
        <p>Most small businesses have Google Analytics installed on their website. Far fewer actually use it. They see the dashboard, feel overwhelmed by the data, and close the tab. GA4 — Google's current analytics platform — is more powerful than its predecessor, but it takes some setup to make it useful. Here is the practical guide to getting GA4 working for your business, from first install to a monthly review routine that takes less than 20 minutes.</p>
        <h2>Why GA4 matters for your business</h2>
        <p>In 2023, Google retired Universal Analytics, its previous platform. GA4 uses a different data model built on events rather than sessions, which means it tracks user behaviour in more detail and across devices more accurately. More importantly for small businesses: it is free, and it tells you where your customers are coming from, what they do on your site, and whether they take action. Without it, you are running a business with no feedback loop on your most important sales channel.</p>
        <h2>How to set up GA4 in under 30 minutes</h2>
        <p>Go to analytics.google.com and sign in with your Google account. Create a new GA4 property, enter your website URL, and set your time zone and currency to match your location. Under Data Collection, create a Web Data Stream and copy the Measurement ID — it starts with G- followed by a string of letters and numbers.</p>
        <p>On WordPress, install the free Google Site Kit plugin and connect your Analytics account through it; it handles the tracking code automatically. On other platforms, paste the Global Site Tag into the head section of every page, or use Google Tag Manager to deploy it without touching code. Verify it is working: visit your own website, then check the Realtime report in GA4. If you see at least one active user, data is flowing correctly.</p>
        <h2>The five reports that actually matter</h2>
        <p>GA4 has dozens of reports. Most small businesses need five. <strong>Acquisition</strong> shows where visitors come from: organic search, social media, direct traffic, or referrals from other sites. <strong>Engagement</strong> shows which pages people read and how long they stay. <strong>Conversions</strong> tracks the specific actions you care about, such as form submissions or phone number clicks. <strong>Demographics</strong> reveals the location, age range, and device type of your visitors. And <strong>Realtime</strong> lets you watch live activity, useful when checking that a new campaign or post is driving traffic.</p>
        <p>Everything else in GA4 is advanced segmentation that you will not need until your traffic volume justifies it. Start with these five and review them consistently before adding complexity.</p>
        <h2>Setting up conversion tracking</h2>
        <p>This is the step most small businesses skip, and it is the most important one. By default, GA4 automatically tracks some events — scrolls, outbound clicks, file downloads. But it does not know which events matter to your business.</p>
        <p>If your goal is form submissions, mark your thank-you page as a conversion event or use Google Tag Manager to fire an event when the form submits. If it is phone calls, create a conversion event for clicks on your phone number link. In GA4, go to Configure, then Events, find the events relevant to your goals, and toggle them as conversions. This single step transforms raw website activity into actual business data.</p>
        <h2>Common GA4 mistakes that skew your data</h2>
        <p><strong>Not filtering your own traffic.</strong> Every time you visit your own website, you are counted as a user, which inflates numbers and distorts your data. In GA4, go to Admin, Data Streams, then Configure Tag Settings, and define your IP address as internal traffic to exclude it from reports.</p>
        <p><strong>Installing analytics too late.</strong> GA4 has no retroactive capability — it collects data only from the moment it is installed. On a new site, add GA4 before launch so your baseline starts on day one. Installing it three months after launch means those first three months are gone forever.</p>
        <p><strong>Watching sessions instead of conversions.</strong> Traffic numbers feel meaningful but rarely tell you whether the site is working. Focus on conversion rate — the percentage of visitors who take the action you want — rather than raw visitor counts. A site with 500 monthly visitors and a 4% conversion rate is more valuable than one with 2,000 visitors and a 0.5% rate.</p>
        <h2>A 15-minute monthly review routine</h2>
        <p>Open GA4 on the same day each month and check three things. First, where is traffic growing or declining compared to last month, and why? A spike in organic traffic often means a page is starting to rank on Google; a drop in direct traffic might mean your offline marketing has slowed. Second, which pages have the lowest engagement rates? A service page with a high bounce rate and low engagement usually signals a messaging or design problem worth addressing. Third, how many conversions happened, and which channels drove them?</p>
        <p>Over time, this data shapes every decision: which content to produce, which pages to improve, which channels to invest in. A website without analytics is a guessing game. With 15 minutes a month and GA4 set up correctly, it becomes a system you can actually improve.</p>
        <p>Every site built through our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> includes GA4 installed and configured from day one, with conversion tracking set up for your contact form so you start collecting meaningful data immediately.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'web-design-pricing-guide',
      title: "How Much Does a Website Cost in the Philippines in 2026?",
      category: 'Strategy',
      date: '03 Oct 2026',
      readTime: '8 min read',
      author: 'Reancirl Balaba',
      authorRole: 'Co-Founder',
      authorPhoto: 'https://fazeddigital.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/117749302_4540601115965165_6039017566881807039_n-450x450.jpg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1498050108023-c5249f4df085?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Website prices in the Philippines run from about ₱15,000 to ₱800,000+. See what drives the cost, the hidden fees, and how to compare quotes.",
      content: `
        <p>A small business website in the Philippines costs anywhere from about ₱15,000 for a template site to ₱250,000 or more for a custom agency build. Published price guides put most freelancer and small-agency work between ₱50,000 and ₱250,000. Our own fixed-price packages at Fazed Digital start at ₱19,999 for a 5-page custom site, live in 7 business days. This guide explains why quotes vary so much, what to budget for after launch, and how to compare offers side by side.</p>
        <h2>The short answer</h2>
        <p>There is no single price for "a website", because two quotes for a "5-page business site" can describe very different amounts of work. As a rough map of the market, <a href="https://designbluemanila.com/blogs/how-much-does-a-website-cost-in-the-philippines-an-honest-breakdown">Designblue Manila's 2026 cost breakdown</a> puts freelancer and template work at ₱50,000 to ₱100,000, small agencies at ₱100,000 to ₱250,000, and boutique or enterprise agencies at ₱250,000 to ₱800,000 and up. Their own FAQ mentions templated quotes as low as ₱15,000. Package studios publish lower starting points: <a href="https://www.starfi.sh">Starfish</a>, for example, lists website packages from ₱28,000, ₱45,000 and ₱60,000.</p>
        <p>Those are other studios' figures, not ours, and every one of them says "from". The useful question is not "what is the average price?" but "what am I getting for this price, and what will it cost me after launch?"</p>
        <h2>What drives the cost of a website</h2>
        <p>Almost every quote you get is built from the same six things. Once you can see them, any quote becomes easier to read.</p>
        <p><strong>Custom design or a template.</strong> A template is a ready-made layout with your logo and text dropped in. It is fast and cheap, and it looks like thousands of other sites. Custom design means someone designs your pages around your business and your customers. That takes more hours, so it costs more.</p>
        <p><strong>Number of pages.</strong> A home page, about page, services page and contact page is a very different job from a 20-page site with a page for every service and branch. More pages means more design, more writing and more testing.</p>
        <p><strong>Features.</strong> A contact form is simple. Online booking, an online shop, a member login or a client portal are small software projects of their own. They are usually the biggest jump in price, because they need building, testing and looking after.</p>
        <p><strong>Content.</strong> Who writes the text and who takes the photos? If you supply everything, the price stays lower. If the studio writes your copy, sources images or runs a photo shoot, expect that to show up in the quote.</p>
        <p><strong>SEO and tracking.</strong> A site that Google can read properly needs page titles, descriptions, a clean structure, fast loading and tools like Google Analytics and Search Console set up. Some quotes include this. Some leave it out and sell it later as a separate service.</p>
        <p><strong>Who does the work, and how fast.</strong> A solo freelancer, a small studio and a large agency have very different costs to cover. Timelines matter too: a fixed scope with a short deadline can keep the price down, while open-ended projects with unlimited changes tend to cost more.</p>
        <h2>Typical price tiers in the Philippines</h2>
        <p>Using the published ranges above, here is what you can usually expect at each level. Treat these as a guide, not a rule. Prices change between cities, studios and years.</p>
        <p><strong>Template sites (roughly ₱15,000 to ₱50,000).</strong> A theme with your logo, colours and text. Often built on WordPress or a site builder. Fine for a business that just needs to show up online, less useful if you want the site to bring in enquiries on its own.</p>
        <p><strong>Freelancer and small studio work (roughly ₱50,000 to ₱250,000).</strong> More design input, a proper page structure, and usually some SEO set-up. Designblue's FAQ puts a basic five-to-ten page business site at ₱100,000 to ₱250,000.</p>
        <p><strong>Agency and custom builds (₱250,000 and up).</strong> Strategy workshops, fully custom design and development, and features like e-commerce, bookings or member areas. Often paired with a monthly support agreement.</p>
        <h2>Our prices, for comparison</h2>
        <p>We publish our prices so you don't have to guess. These are Fazed Digital's own fixed-price 7-day packages for Philippine businesses:</p>
        <p><strong>Starter, ₱19,999.</strong> 5 custom-designed pages, mobile-responsive layout, on-page SEO set-up, a contact form and 2 revision rounds. For new businesses getting online.</p>
        <p><strong>Growth, ₱34,999.</strong> 8 custom-designed pages, a custom design system, advanced on-page SEO, a blog and CMS, Google Analytics and Search Console, and 3 revision rounds. For growing small businesses.</p>
        <p><strong>Pro, ₱59,999.</strong> 10+ custom pages, full brand integration, performance tuning for loads under 2 seconds, e-commerce ready, priority support and unlimited revisions. For businesses ready to scale.</p>
        <p>Every package is custom-designed, not a template, and goes live in 7 business days. The condition: you send everything we need on Day 1 and your feedback within 24 hours at each review. If we miss the date on our side, you get 20% off the project fee. Extra pages are ₱2,500 each. Booking systems, membership portals and custom e-commerce are scoped separately, and you get that price upfront. You can pay by GCash, Maya, bank transfer (BPI or Metrobank) or Wise. See everything included on the <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/lp/7-day-website/ph">7-day website packages in pesos</a>.</p>
        <p>Why can a fixed package cost less than a typical agency quote? Because the scope is set before we start: a known number of pages, a known number of revision rounds and a 7-day schedule. That works for most small business sites. It is not the right fit for a complex system, and we will tell you if yours needs a custom quote instead.</p>
        <h2>Hidden costs to budget for</h2>
        <p>The build price is only part of what a website costs you. Ask about each of these before you sign.</p>
        <p><strong>Domain name.</strong> Your web address (yourbusiness.com or yourbusiness.ph) is renewed every year. Make sure it is registered in your name, not the developer's. If someone else owns the domain, they effectively own your online address.</p>
        <p><strong>Hosting.</strong> Hosting is the server your site lives on, usually paid monthly or yearly. Cheap shared hosting can make a good site slow. Ask which host the studio uses, what it costs to renew, and whose account it sits in.</p>
        <p><strong>Maintenance.</strong> Websites need software updates, backups and security checks, especially on WordPress. You can do this yourself, or pay someone to do it. Our care plans start at ₱3,500 a month and cover updates, backups, security monitoring, uptime checks and small content changes. A plan is optional, but skipping maintenance completely is a common way for sites to get hacked or break after an update.</p>
        <p><strong>Content changes and extra pages.</strong> Find out what a new page or a text change costs after launch. If you can edit the site yourself through a CMS, small changes cost you nothing but time.</p>
        <p><strong>Paid tools and licences.</strong> Premium themes, plugins, booking tools, email accounts and payment gateways can each carry their own fees. A good quote lists them so you are not surprised later.</p>
        <h2>How to compare website quotes</h2>
        <p>Put your quotes side by side and check each one against these questions. A studio you can trust will answer all of them clearly.</p>
        <p><strong>What exactly is included?</strong> Number of pages, features, revision rounds and SEO set-up should be written down. "Complete website" is not a scope.</p>
        <p><strong>Is it custom or a template?</strong> Both can be the right choice. Just make sure you know which one you are paying for.</p>
        <p><strong>Who owns the site?</strong> The domain, hosting, logins, design files and content should be yours once you have paid. Avoid contracts that lock you in.</p>
        <p><strong>How long will it take, and what do you need from me?</strong> A timeline only means something if you know what you must provide, and when.</p>
        <p><strong>What happens after launch?</strong> Ask about maintenance, support and the cost of changes. Can your team update the site themselves?</p>
        <p><strong>Who does the work?</strong> Will the people you speak to actually design and build your site, or is it passed on to someone else?</p>
        <p><strong>How do I pay?</strong> Most studios take a deposit before starting. Ours is 50%, as set out in our terms. Know the payment schedule before work begins.</p>
        <p>Not sure your current site is worth rebuilding at all? Book a <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/audit">free 15-minute website check</a>. We look at your site with you and point out the 3 fixes that would bring you the most calls and enquiries. No site yet? Book anyway.</p>
        <h2>Buying from outside the Philippines?</h2>
        <p>If your business is in the USA, Australia or elsewhere, the same packages are priced in US dollars: Starter $499, Growth $899 and Pro $1,499, with extra pages at $79 and care plans from $99 a month. Custom websites and systems are quoted after a discovery call and typically run $5,000 to $20,000, depending on scope. Overseas clients pay by Wise or bank transfer. Most of our case studies are for clients in the USA and Australia, and we work remotely with clear milestones, regular video check-ins and written updates. See the <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/lp/7-day-website">7-day website packages in USD</a>.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>How much is a simple website in the Philippines?</strong> A simple 5-page business site can cost from around ₱15,000 for a template to ₱100,000 or more from an agency, based on published price guides. Our own custom 5-page Starter package is a fixed ₱19,999, live in 7 business days.</p>
        <p><strong>Is ₱19,999 the full price?</strong> It is the fixed price for the Starter package as listed. Extra pages are ₱2,500 each, and features like bookings or an online shop are quoted separately before work starts. Maintenance is optional and starts at ₱3,500 a month.</p>
        <p><strong>Who owns the website when it is done?</strong> You do. Once it is paid for, the domain, hosting, logins, design files and content are yours. There is no lock-in contract.</p>
        <p><strong>Can we meet in person?</strong> Yes, if you are in Iligan City or Cagayan de Oro. We do not have a client office yet, so we meet at your business or somewhere convenient. We are happy to talk in Bisaya, Tagalog or English. Read more about <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/web-design-iligan-city">web design in Iligan City</a>.</p>
      `,
