    {
      slug: 'rebranding-when-and-how',
      title: "When to Rebrand (And When You Really Shouldn't)",
      category: 'Branding',
      date: '25 Sep 2026',
      readTime: '4 min read',
      author: 'Lord Arcamo',
      authorRole: 'Founder',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-lord.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1434626881859-194d67b2b86f?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Rebranding is expensive, disruptive, and often unnecessary. Here's how to honestly assess whether your brand needs a refresh — or whether the real problem is something else entirely.",
      content: `
        <p>Rebrand when your business has genuinely changed, your audience has changed or your brand is causing real confusion. Do not rebrand because you are bored, sales are down or a competitor did. Most businesses that think they need a rebrand actually need a smaller refresh, better marketing or a clearer offer.</p>
        <p>Rebranding for the wrong reasons wastes money and throws away recognition you have spent years building. So before you brief a designer, work out what problem you are really trying to solve.</p>
        <h2>Legitimate reasons to rebrand</h2>
        <p><strong>Your business has fundamentally changed.</strong> You have moved into a different market, expanded your services significantly or merged with another company. The brand no longer describes what you do or who you serve. This is the clearest signal that a rebrand is warranted.</p>
        <p><strong>Your audience has changed.</strong> You started with small local clients and now you are pitching enterprise contracts. Your look and voice need to match where you are going, not where you have been.</p>
        <p><strong>Your brand undersells your work.</strong> The service has moved upmarket, but the brand still says budget. Cleen &amp; Green, a cleaning company in the Philippines, is a good example. They delivered a premium, eco-conscious service, but their old identity and website did not show it, so we built them <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/work/02">a full brand identity and a new WordPress site</a> around those values.</p>
        <p><strong>Your brand is creating real friction.</strong> Prospects regularly misunderstand what you do. Your name keeps getting confused with a competitor's. You have entered a market where your name carries no meaning, or the wrong one. These are specific problems that a rebrand can solve.</p>
        <h2>Bad reasons to rebrand</h2>
        <p><strong>You are bored with it.</strong> You see your logo every day, and you are tired of it. Your customers are not. Brand fatigue is an internal problem, so do not solve it at the expense of the recognition you have built.</p>
        <p><strong>Sales are down.</strong> A rebrand is not a sales strategy. If revenue is falling, the cause is more likely to be your offer, pricing, sales process or marketing channels than your logo. Fix the real problem first.</p>
        <p><strong>A competitor rebranded.</strong> Their reasons are not yours. Do not let their decision push you into an expensive exercise that does not serve your business.</p>
        <p><strong>You have a new manager or partner.</strong> New people often want to leave their mark. That is natural, but it needs to be backed by a business reason, not a personal preference.</p>
        <h2>The middle path: a brand refresh</h2>
        <p>Most businesses do not need a full rebrand. They need a refresh: updating the typography, tightening the <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/blog/brand-colours-guide">colour palette</a>, modernising the logo without abandoning it and clarifying the brand voice. This keeps the recognition you have while bringing the brand up to date. It is faster and cheaper than starting from zero, and usually the smarter move.</p>
        <p>A simple rule: if customers already know and like your name, keep the name and refresh the look. If the name itself is the problem, that is a true rebrand, and you should plan for it properly.</p>
        <h2>Before you decide, test the hypothesis</h2>
        <p>Ask ten customers, not colleagues or family, what words come to mind when they think of your brand. Ask what they think you do. Ask if they would recommend you. Then ask a few people who chose a competitor why they did. Their answers will show whether a brand problem actually exists. If it does not, spend your rebrand budget on marketing instead.</p>
        <h2>The costs people forget</h2>
        <p>The designer's invoice is only part of the bill. A rebrand touches everything that carries your name or logo: signage, vehicles, uniforms, packaging, printed stock, your website, email signatures, social profiles, invoices, proposals and directory listings. List them all before you commit, and price the changes.</p>
        <p>A name change adds more. You may need a new domain and new email addresses, and you may need to update your business registration. On the website side, set up redirects from every old URL to its new home, or you risk losing the search rankings you have earned. Update your Google Business Profile too, and check whether a trademark search is needed before you fall in love with a new name.</p>
        <h2>How to roll it out</h2>
        <p>Pick one launch date and make the switch as complete as you can on that day, starting with the things customers see most: website, social profiles and Google Business Profile. Brief your team first, so nobody is surprised when a customer asks about it. Then tell your customers why you changed, in plain language, and reassure them about what is staying the same. Finish the slower items, such as signage and vehicles, on a written schedule so that the old brand does not linger for years.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>How long does a rebrand take?</strong> It depends on scope. Our brand identity projects typically take 4 to 6 weeks, and rolling the new identity out across signage and print can take longer.</p>
        <p><strong>Will a rebrand hurt my SEO?</strong> It can if your domain or URLs change without redirects. If you keep the same domain and URLs and only change the look, the risk is low.</p>
        <p>Not sure if you need a rebrand or a refresh? <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/contact">Tell us what has changed in your business</a> and we will give you a straight answer.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'landing-page-conversion-design',
      title: "The Anatomy of a High-Converting Landing Page",
      category: 'Design',
      date: '18 Sep 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Lord Arcamo',
      authorRole: 'Founder',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-lord.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1581291518857-4d27a540d9f2?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Most landing pages fail at the same predictable points. Here's the structure that works — backed by what we've seen across dozens of client campaigns.",
      content: `
        <p>A high-converting landing page has one job: get one specific visitor to take one specific action. The structure that works is a clear headline, proof, a plain offer, honest answers to objections and one repeated call to action. Most landing pages fail because they try to do too much at once.</p>
        <p>A landing page is not there to explain everything you do or show your full portfolio. One page, one goal. Here is how each part of it should work, in the order your visitor meets it.</p>
        <h2>Above the fold: the only section that matters first</h2>
        <p>Before your visitor scrolls, they have already made a judgment, usually within seconds. The top of the page must answer three questions at once: What is this? Who is it for? Why should I care? Your headline covers what and for whom. Your subheadline covers why. Your primary button covers what to do next.</p>
        <p>The most common mistake here is leading with your company name and tagline. Nobody cares yet. Lead with the outcome the visitor gets, not who you are. "Get a website that brings in enquiries" tells people far more than "Welcome to Fazed Digital".</p>
        <p>The top of the page must also match whatever brought the visitor there. If your ad promises same-day aircon repair, the headline should say same-day aircon repair. When the words change between the ad and the page, people assume they have landed in the wrong place. If you run ads for different services, give each one its own page.</p>
        <h2>Social proof placed before the ask</h2>
        <p>Trust must come before commitment. A visitor who does not trust you will not act, no matter how good your offer sounds. Place real testimonials, client logos, case study callouts or review ratings early, ideally in the first scroll and before your main button.</p>
        <p>Specific proof works better than generic praise. A testimonial that names the problem, what you did and what changed says much more than "Great to work with!" Our guide to <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/blog/client-testimonials-strategy">collecting client testimonials</a> shows how to get them.</p>
        <p>Use only proof you can stand behind. If you are new and have no reviews yet, do not invent any. Show real work, your process, your team and any guarantee you offer instead. Those are honest reasons to trust you.</p>
        <h2>The offer section: clarity over creativity</h2>
        <p>Explain what the visitor gets, in plain language, in three to five short points. Use the visitor's language, not yours, and describe outcomes rather than features. "A website live in 4 weeks" is clearer than "Rapid deployment methodology." Every word that needs explaining is a word that loses people.</p>
        <p>Show the price if you can. A visible price filters out poor-fit enquiries and answers the first question on most people's minds. If you cannot give a fixed price, give a starting point or explain what changes it. Say what happens right after someone clicks too, because uncertainty stops people from acting.</p>
        <h2>Objection handling built into the page</h2>
        <p>Every visitor has silent objections: Is this too expensive? Will it work for my industry? What if I am not happy? Answer these directly in an FAQ section or in your copy. Naming the objection and answering it honestly builds the trust that gets people to click.</p>
        <p>Put the strongest objection first, not the easiest one. To find out what those are, read your sales emails and ask your team which questions come up before people say no. Those are the questions your page must answer.</p>
        <h2>One CTA, repeated strategically</h2>
        <p>On most landing pages, your call to action should appear about three times: at the top, after the proof section and at the bottom. It should say the same thing each time, not "Learn More" in one place and "Get Started" in another. Make the button describe the action: "Book a Free Strategy Call" is stronger than "Submit."</p>
        <p>Remove anything else that competes with it. That usually means taking out your full navigation menu and any links that send people elsewhere. Keep the form short too. Every field you add gives people another reason to stop, as we explain in our list of <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/blog/ux-mistakes-killing-conversions">common UX mistakes</a>.</p>
        <h2>Speed and mobile: the invisible conversion killers</h2>
        <p>A slow landing page loses people before they read a word, and that is especially painful when you are paying for each click. StatCounter's global figures put phones at more than half of web traffic, and paid social traffic is often even more mobile. Design and test the page on a phone first. Run it through Google's PageSpeed Insights before you spend anything on ads.</p>
        <h2>A real page to pull apart</h2>
        <p>You can see this structure on our own <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/lp/7-day-website">7-day website page</a>. It has one offer, published prices, a day-by-day process and a guarantee with its conditions spelled out. The FAQ starts with the strongest objection, which is whether the seven days are guaranteed. It is not the only way to build a landing page, but it gives you something concrete to compare yours against.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>How long should a landing page be?</strong> As long as it takes to answer the visitor's questions. A simple, low-cost offer can be short. A bigger or more expensive decision needs more proof and more answers.</p>
        <p><strong>Should a landing page have a navigation menu?</strong> Usually not. Removing the menu keeps attention on one action. Keep a small footer with your contact details and privacy policy, so the page still feels legitimate.</p>
        <p><strong>How do I test what works?</strong> Change one thing at a time, starting with the headline, and wait until enough people have seen each version before you judge it. On low traffic, ask real customers what confused them instead.</p>
        <p>Want to know where your page is losing people? <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/audit">Book a free 15-minute website check</a> and we will go through it with you on Google Meet.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'wordpress-vs-custom-build',
      title: "WordPress vs Custom Build: Which Is Right for Your Business?",
      category: 'Development',
      date: '10 Sep 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Justin Sumaya',
      authorRole: 'Head of Development',
      authorPhoto: 'https://fazeddigital.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/151652330_3908937289163068_8338977159966182887_n-450x450.jpg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1461749280684-dccba630e2f6?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "It's not a technical decision — it's a business one. Here's the honest breakdown of when WordPress wins and when a custom build is worth the investment.",
      content: `
        <p>Choose WordPress if you run a standard business site and want to edit it yourself. Choose a custom build if speed is a priority, your needs are unusual or the website is itself the product. WordPress vs custom build is a business decision, not a technical one: it comes down to your budget, your team and your next two years.</p>
        <p>We build on both WordPress and custom stacks such as Astro and Next.js, and we recommend whichever fits the job. Here is the honest breakdown.</p>
        <h2>The case for WordPress</h2>
        <p>W3Techs, which tracks the technology websites use, puts WordPress at around 40% of all websites. There are good reasons for that: it is mature, well supported and editable by non-technical people. If you need to add blog posts, change service descriptions or update team pages regularly, its admin area is easy to learn.</p>
        <p>The plugin ecosystem is huge. Need a booking system, a contact form with conditional logic or a membership area? There is a plugin for it, and it usually costs far less than a custom equivalent. Developers are also easy to find, so you are not tied to one supplier.</p>
        <p>A Framing Company, a contractor in Utah, is a good example of a strong fit. We built them <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/work/01">a WordPress site around their project portfolio</a>, with service pages for wood framing, concrete and roofing, and a CMS their team can edit themselves.</p>
        <h2>Where WordPress falls short</h2>
        <p>Performance is WordPress's biggest liability when it is set up carelessly. Heavy themes, page builders and a long list of plugins can lead to poor Core Web Vitals and slow mobile loading. A well-built WordPress site can be fast, but it takes deliberate work and decent hosting.</p>
        <p>It also needs regular care. Plugins conflict, updates can break things and popular software attracts automated attacks. Budget for <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/blog/website-maintenance-guide">ongoing maintenance</a> from day one, not as an afterthought.</p>
        <p>WordPress can also become a constraint. When you need something it does not do well, such as complex data relationships, real-time features or highly customised user journeys, you end up fighting the platform rather than building with it.</p>
        <h2>The case for a custom build</h2>
        <p>A custom build starts from nothing and includes only what you need. That means less unused code, no plugin weight and complete design freedom. For businesses where the website is a core product, such as SaaS platforms, marketplaces and systems with complex logic, custom is often the only sensible option.</p>
        <p>Two of our projects show the difference. 8Drive needed one system for bookings, fleet availability, customer records and chauffeur scheduling, and that took a 10-week custom build. Canwell, an Australian health and wellness platform, needed a public website and a separate admin portal working together, which we delivered in 8 weeks. Neither would have been a good fit for a stack of plugins. You can see more of this work on our <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/australia">page for Australian businesses</a>.</p>
        <p>Static site generators such as Astro also make fast marketing sites easier to achieve, because they send very little code to the browser. If your pages must load quickly on phones and mobile data, that head start is valuable.</p>
        <h2>What custom costs you that WordPress does not</h2>
        <p>The trade-offs are real, and you should hear them before you sign. A custom build costs more upfront and takes longer. Our custom projects typically run from $5,000 to $20,000 after a discovery call, while our 7-day packages start from $499, or ₱19,999 in the Philippines.</p>
        <p>Editing can also be less familiar. A custom site needs a CMS connected to it if you want to change content yourself, so make sure this is in the scope. You also depend more on developers for new features, which makes documentation and code ownership important. Before you start, confirm in writing that you will own the code once it is paid for.</p>
        <h2>A side-by-side check</h2>
        <p><strong>Budget:</strong> if money is tight, WordPress or a fixed-scope package usually wins. <strong>Editing:</strong> if several non-technical staff publish every week, WordPress is hard to beat. <strong>Speed:</strong> if performance is a top priority, a static build has the easier path. <strong>Security:</strong> a static site has less to attack, while WordPress needs regular updates. <strong>Unusual features:</strong> if your process does not fit an existing plugin, go custom. <strong>Time:</strong> a standard site can be live in days or weeks, while a platform takes months.</p>
        <h2>The honest recommendation</h2>
        <p>For most small business brochure sites, service businesses and content blogs: WordPress, built well, with quality hosting. For e-commerce beyond basic WooCommerce: look at purpose-built platforms such as Shopify first. For marketing sites where performance and SEO are priorities, or any business where the website is the product: a custom build. When in doubt, ask what you will need to change in 12 months. The answer usually makes the decision obvious.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>Is WordPress bad for SEO?</strong> No. Google does not rank a site lower for using WordPress. Slow pages and thin content cause the problems, and those can happen on any platform.</p>
        <p><strong>Can I edit a custom website myself?</strong> Yes, if it is built with a CMS. Ask for that at the start, along with a handover session for your team.</p>
        <p><strong>Can I start on WordPress and go custom later?</strong> Yes. Many businesses do. Your content can move across, though the design and features will need rebuilding, and old URLs will need redirects.</p>
        <p>Still unsure which way to go? <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/contact">Tell us what your website needs to do</a> and we will recommend the right fit, not just the one we like best.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'keyword-research-small-business',
      title: "Keyword Research for Small Businesses: Find the Terms That Actually Win",
      category: 'SEO',
      date: '01 Sep 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Russel Heyrana',
      authorRole: 'Marketing Director',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-russel.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1460925895917-afdab827c52f?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Ranking for a keyword nobody searches is pointless. Ranking for one you can't compete on is worse. Here's how to find the terms your business can realistically own.",
      content: `
        <p>Keyword research for a small business means finding the phrases your customers really type, then choosing the ones you can realistically rank for and that lead to sales. Skip the obvious big terms. Use customer language, free tools and a quick look at who already ranks to build a short list of winnable keywords.</p>
        <p>The most common mistake is going after broad terms such as "web design", "accounting services" or "real estate", then wondering why you are stuck on page 8. Those terms are dominated by companies with big budgets and years of authority. You win by finding the searches your ideal customers make that your competitors have not fully answered.</p>
        <h2>Start with your customer's language, not yours</h2>
        <p>Your customers do not search the way you talk about your business. An accountant's client does not search "bookkeeping services for SMEs". They search "how to do BIR filing for small business" or "accountant for online sellers Philippines". The gap between industry language and customer language is where keyword opportunities live.</p>
        <p>The fastest way to close this gap is to talk to five actual customers and ask what they searched for before they found you. Their exact phrases are your starting point. Then type those phrases into Google and note the autocomplete suggestions and the "People also ask" box. These surface real search behaviour with no software required.</p>
        <h2>The keyword matrix: volume vs. competition vs. intent</h2>
        <p>Every keyword sits on three dimensions. <strong>Volume</strong>: how many people search for it each month. <strong>Competition</strong>: how hard it is to rank for it. <strong>Intent</strong>: what the searcher is trying to do, whether that is research, compare or buy.</p>
        <p>The sweet spot for small businesses is low-to-medium competition, clear buying intent and enough volume to matter. A keyword with 200 monthly searches, low competition and people ready to hire is worth far more than a keyword with 10,000 monthly searches dominated by major brands.</p>
        <p>In small cities, tools often show little or no volume for phrases that real people do search. Do not throw those away. If customers tell you they used a phrase, it counts, whatever the tool says.</p>
        <h2>Free tools that are good enough to start</h2>
        <p>Google Search Console shows what you already appear for, so start there. Open the Performance report and look for queries with plenty of impressions but few clicks. Those are your quickest wins. Google's Keyword Planner, free with a Google Ads account, gives volume ranges. Ubersuggest's free tier provides difficulty scores and related terms. Ahrefs and Semrush are more powerful, but you do not need them until you have exhausted the free options. Most small businesses can build a strong keyword list without spending a peso on tools.</p>
        <h2>Check the results page with your own eyes</h2>
        <p>A difficulty score is only an estimate. The best test is free: search the keyword and look at page one. If you see national brands, government sites and big directories, it will be hard. If you see thin pages, old posts, local competitors and Facebook pages, you have a real chance.</p>
        <p>Note what type of page is ranking too. If the results are all guides, a sales page will struggle. If they are all service pages, a blog post will struggle. Google is showing you what searchers want, so make that kind of page.</p>
        <h2>Long-tail keywords: where small businesses actually win</h2>
        <p>Long-tail keywords are longer, more specific phrases, such as "affordable web design for restaurants in Cebu" rather than "web design Philippines". Each one has lower volume, but they are far easier to rank for, and the people searching them are usually further along in the buying process. Building content around 20 well-chosen long-tail keywords will usually do more for you than chasing 3 competitive head terms.</p>
        <h2>A worked example you can copy</h2>
        <p>Say you are an accountant in Davao. "Accountant Philippines" is too broad. Your customer interviews turn up "accountant for online sellers", "how to register a small business with the BIR" and "bookkeeper Davao". Now open a spreadsheet with five columns: keyword, rough volume, who ranks now, intent, and the page that will target it.</p>
        <p>"Bookkeeper Davao" has buying intent and local competitors, so it goes on a service page. "How to register a small business with the BIR" is a research query, so it suits a guide that links to your services. "Accountant for online sellers" could be a page of its own. Fifteen to twenty rows like these will keep you busy for months.</p>
        <h2>Turning keywords into content</h2>
        <p>Each group of related keywords should map to one piece of content: a service page, a blog post or a location page. Do not create separate pages for near-identical phrases, because they will compete with each other. The page should answer the searcher's question completely and use the keyword naturally in the title, H1 and first paragraph. Our <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/blog/on-page-seo-checklist">on-page SEO checklist</a> covers the details, and if your site is brand new, read <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/blog/seo-for-new-websites">SEO for new websites</a> first. Thin content that merely mentions a keyword ranks nowhere. Thorough content that really helps the searcher is what earns and holds rankings over time.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>How many keywords should I target per page?</strong> One main keyword and a few close variations. Different services or topics need their own pages.</p>
        <p><strong>How long until I rank?</strong> It depends on the competition and the age of your site, but SEO usually takes months, not weeks. Local long-tail terms tend to move first.</p>
        <p>Want to know if Google can read your pages properly? <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/audit">Book a free 15-minute website check</a> and we will look at your site with you.</p>
      `,
    },
