      slug: 'local-seo-small-business-guide',
      title: "Local SEO for Small Businesses: How to Get Found on Google Maps",
      category: 'SEO',
      date: '02 Oct 2026',
      readTime: '4 min read',
      author: 'Russel Heyrana',
      authorRole: 'Marketing Director',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-russel.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1517180102446-f3ece451e9d8?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Most small businesses lose customers to competitors simply because they can't be found online. Here's the local SEO playbook that gets you on the map — literally.",
      content: `
        <p>If you run a local business and you're not showing up in Google's Map Pack, you're handing customers to your competitors every single day. Local SEO isn't complicated — but most businesses are doing it wrong, or not doing it at all.</p>
        <h2>Why local SEO is different from regular SEO</h2>
        <p>Traditional SEO is about ranking for broad search terms. Local SEO is about ranking for searches that happen near you — "web designer in Iligan City," "best coffee shop near me," "HVAC repair Manila." These searches have immediate commercial intent: the person is ready to buy, call, or visit.</p>
        <p>Google's algorithm for local results weighs three factors: <strong>relevance</strong> (does your business match what they searched for?), <strong>distance</strong> (how close are you to the searcher?), and <strong>prominence</strong> (how well-known and trusted is your business online?). Most businesses only focus on relevance and ignore the other two.</p>
        <h2>Step one: Claim and complete your Google Business Profile</h2>
        <p>Your Google Business Profile (formerly Google My Business) is the single most impactful thing you can optimise for local search. If it's not claimed, your competitor probably already has an advantage. If it's claimed but incomplete, you're leaving ranking signals on the table.</p>
        <p>Complete every field: business name, category (be specific — not just "Restaurant" but "Filipino Restaurant"), address, phone number, website, hours, and attributes. Add at least 10 photos — Google rewards profiles with frequent, quality images. Write a 750-character business description with your primary keyword naturally included. Post an update at least once a week; Google treats active profiles as more trustworthy.</p>
        <h2>Step two: Build consistent citations</h2>
        <p>A citation is any online mention of your business name, address, and phone number (NAP). Consistency is critical — Google cross-references these signals to verify you are who you say you are. If your address appears differently across directories, it creates confusion that suppresses your rankings.</p>
        <p>Start with the major platforms: Facebook, Yelp, Foursquare, Bing Places, Apple Maps, and any industry-specific directories. In the Philippines, also prioritise local platforms and industry association directories relevant to your sector. Aim for 20–30 consistent citations in the first three months. Use the exact same format every time — "St." versus "Street" can create a mismatch.</p>
        <h2>Step three: Get more reviews — and respond to every one</h2>
        <p>Reviews are one of the most heavily weighted local ranking factors. A business with 80 reviews and a 4.6-star average will consistently outrank a business with 10 reviews and a 5-star average. Both volume and recency matter — a burst of reviews two years ago is worth less than a steady flow today.</p>
        <p>Build a simple review request process: after a positive interaction, send a follow-up message with a direct link to your Google review page. Don't ask for "5-star reviews" — ask for "honest feedback." And respond to every review, positive or negative. Google interprets review responses as an engagement signal, and potential customers read them before they decide to call.</p>
        <h2>Step four: Optimise your website for local keywords</h2>
        <p>Your website needs to tell Google exactly where you operate and what you do. Create a dedicated location page — or at minimum, include location signals on your homepage — with your city and region in the page title, H1 heading, and first paragraph. Embed a Google Map. Add LocalBusiness schema markup: this structured data explicitly tells search engines your name, address, phone, hours, and geographic service area.</p>
        <p>For businesses serving multiple areas, create a separate page for each location. Thin, templated content won't rank — each page needs genuine, useful information about the specific area and how your business serves it.</p>
        <h2>How long before you see results?</h2>
        <p>Most businesses see meaningful movement in local rankings within 60–90 days of a complete optimisation. A fully built-out Google Business Profile with regular posts and new reviews typically produces visible results fastest. Full citation consistency and on-page optimisation take longer but compound over time — the longer you maintain them, the harder they are for competitors to displace.</p>
        <p>Local SEO isn't a campaign with an end date. It's an ongoing system. The businesses that treat it that way are the ones that own their local market.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'seo-for-new-websites',
      title: "SEO for New Websites: How to Rank on Google When You're Starting From Zero",
      category: 'SEO',
      date: '27 Aug 2026',
      readTime: '3 min read',
      author: 'Russel Heyrana',
      authorRole: 'Marketing Director',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-russel.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1460925895917-afdab827c52f?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "A brand-new website has no authority, no backlinks and no history. Here's the practical 90-day SEO plan we use to get new small-business sites indexed and ranking.",
      content: `
        <p>Launching a new website feels like opening a shop on a street nobody has heard of. Google does not know you exist yet, it has no reason to trust you, and your competitors have years of head start. The good news: new sites can rank, and often faster than people expect, if the first 90 days are spent on the right things in the right order.</p>
        <h2>Weeks 1–2: Make sure Google can actually find you</h2>
        <p>Before content, before links, confirm the basics. <strong>Set up Google Search Console</strong> and verify your domain. Submit your XML sitemap. Use the URL Inspection tool to request indexing for your homepage and your most important service pages. Check that nothing is accidentally blocking crawlers: a leftover "noindex" tag from staging or an overly strict robots.txt file is one of the most common reasons a new site never appears.</p>
        <p>At the same time, create or claim your <strong>Google Business Profile</strong> if you serve a local area. For many small businesses, the Map Pack will deliver leads long before organic rankings do.</p>
        <h2>Weeks 2–4: Target keywords you can realistically win</h2>
        <p>A new site will not rank for "web design" or "best restaurant" in its first year. It can rank for specific, lower-competition phrases with clear intent. Think "wedding photographer in Cagayan de Oro" rather than "photographer", or "how much does a commercial aircon cleaning cost" rather than "aircon".</p>
        <p>Build a list of 15–25 of these long-tail terms. Each core service gets its own dedicated page targeting one primary phrase. <strong>One page, one intent.</strong> Cramming every service onto a single page is the fastest way to rank for nothing.</p>
        <h2>Weeks 4–8: Publish content that answers real questions</h2>
        <p>Look at the questions customers ask you on the phone, over Messenger and in person. Each recurring question is a potential article. Write the most genuinely useful answer on the internet for that question, with specifics: prices, timelines, steps, mistakes to avoid.</p>
        <p>Consistency matters more than volume. Two strong articles a month for six months beats twelve thin ones in a single burst. Link every article back to the relevant service page so the authority you earn flows to the pages that make money.</p>
        <h2>Weeks 6–12: Earn your first trustworthy links</h2>
        <p>Backlinks remain one of the strongest ranking signals, and new sites have none. Start with the links you can get honestly and easily: local business directories, your chamber of commerce, suppliers and partners who list their clients, industry associations, and local news coverage of anything genuinely newsworthy you do.</p>
        <p>Avoid anyone selling "1,000 backlinks for ₱2,000". Those links are worthless at best and can trigger a penalty at worst. <strong>Ten relevant, real links will do more than a thousand spammy ones.</strong></p>
        <h2>Technical foundations that give you an unfair advantage</h2>
        <p>Many established competitors are running slow, bloated sites built years ago. A new site that loads in under two seconds, passes Core Web Vitals, works perfectly on mobile and uses clean heading structure starts with an edge. Google has said page experience is a tiebreaker; for a newcomer competing on long-tail terms, tiebreakers matter.</p>
        <h2>What to expect, and when</h2>
        <p>Realistically: indexing within days to a couple of weeks, first impressions for long-tail terms within one to two months, and meaningful traffic from organic search in three to six months, depending on competition. Track progress in Search Console by watching impressions, not just clicks. Rising impressions mean Google is testing you, and clicks follow.</p>
        <p>If you are still on the starting line without a fast, SEO-ready site, our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> gives you the technical foundations above from launch day, so your 90-day clock starts immediately.</p>
      `,
