    {
      slug: 'how-to-choose-web-design-agency-philippines',
      title: "How to Choose a Web Design Agency in the Philippines (Without Getting Burned)",
      category: 'Strategy',
      date: '20 Aug 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Reancirl Balaba',
      authorRole: 'Co-Founder',
      authorPhoto: 'https://fazeddigital.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/117749302_4540601115965165_6039017566881807039_n-450x450.jpg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1552664730-d307ca884978?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Portfolios look great, quotes vary wildly, and some agencies vanish after launch. Use this checklist to pick a Philippine web design partner you can trust.",
      content: `
        <p>To choose a web design agency in the Philippines, test their live sites on your phone, find out who will do the work, get ownership of your domain and files in writing, compare quotes by scope, and ask what happens after launch. The best choice is rarely the cheapest quote. It is the one that avoids expensive mistakes.</p>
        <p>A freelancer who disappears halfway. An agency that holds the domain hostage. A site that looks fine on a laptop but breaks on every phone. Ask around and you will hear stories like these. Here is how to evaluate any agency, including us.</p>
        <h2>Look past the portfolio screenshots</h2>
        <p>A portfolio shows what an agency can make look good in a screenshot. It does not show whether those sites load quickly, rank on Google or bring in customers. Ask for <strong>live links</strong>, then open them on your phone using mobile data. Are they fast? Do the forms work? Is the copy clear? Then ask one or two of those clients directly how the project went. A confident agency will usually be happy to connect you.</p>
        <p>Check that the work is really theirs, too. Ask what exactly they did on each project. Did they design it, build it, write it, or all three? Agencies can also show concept work that was never live, which is fine as long as it is labelled clearly.</p>
        <h2>Ask who will actually do the work</h2>
        <p>Some agencies sell the project with senior people, then hand it to a junior or an outsourced team you never meet. That is not automatically bad, but you deserve to know. Ask who your day-to-day contact will be, who designs, who develops and whether any part is subcontracted. <strong>Clarity here predicts how smoothly the project will run.</strong></p>
        <h2>Make sure you will own everything</h2>
        <p>This is the single most important question, and the one most often skipped. Your domain should be registered in your name, on an account you control. You should have admin access to your hosting and your website. Design files, logos and content should belong to you once paid for. If an agency insists on owning the domain "for convenience", walk away. Untangling that later can take months.</p>
        <p>Get this in the contract, not just in a chat. Ask about the templates, plugins and licences used on your site as well. If they run on the agency's own account, find out what happens if you stop working with them.</p>
        <h2>Compare quotes on scope, not price</h2>
        <p>Quotes for a "business website" vary widely, and both the lowest and the highest can be honest. The difference is scope. Ask every agency to itemise: number of pages, custom design or template, copywriting included or not, SEO setup, mobile testing, revisions, training and post-launch support. Lay the quotes side by side and the price gaps usually make sense. For a fuller breakdown of what drives the price, read our <a href="/blog/web-design-pricing-guide">web design pricing guide</a>.</p>
        <p>Check what is not included too. Domain and hosting fees, stock photos, plugin licences, extra revisions and content writing are common add-ons. Ask for the total first-year cost, not only the design fee.</p>
        <h2>Red flags to watch for</h2>
        <p>Be careful if you see any of these: no written contract, 100% payment upfront, vague timelines such as "around one to two months", or no mention of what happens after launch. Be wary of guaranteed first-page rankings as well, because nobody controls Google. The same goes for pressure to sign today, a portfolio with no live links, and a price far below everyone else's with no explanation of what has been left out.</p>
        <p>A deposit is normal. Our standard terms, for example, are a 50% deposit before work starts. Just make sure you know what each payment is for and when the rest is due.</p>
        <h2>Check how they handle timelines and communication</h2>
        <p>Ask for a project timeline with specific milestones. Ask how often you will get updates and on which channel. Ask what they need from you and by when, because client content is a very common cause of delay. A good agency is upfront about that and builds a process around it.</p>
        <p>Location matters less than it used to, as a project can run well on video calls and shared links. If you want to meet in person, ask at the start. We meet clients who are in or near Iligan City, and everyone else works with us online.</p>
        <h2>Ask what happens after launch</h2>
        <p>A website is not a one-time purchase. Plugins need updates, security needs monitoring and your content will change. Find out whether the agency offers maintenance, what it costs and how quickly they respond if the site goes down. An agency that only talks about the build and never about the next twelve months is telling you something.</p>
        <h2>A simple scoring checklist</h2>
        <p>Before deciding, score each agency out of five on: quality of live work, transparency on who does the work, ownership terms, clarity of scope, timeline commitment and post-launch support. The highest score, not the lowest price, is usually the right choice.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>Should I hire a freelancer or an agency?</strong> A good freelancer can suit a small, simple site. An agency usually offers more skills and backup if someone is unavailable. Use the same questions for both.</p>
        <p><strong>What should a website contract include?</strong> Scope, price, payment schedule, timeline, number of revisions, who owns what and what happens if either side ends the project.</p>
        <p>We built our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> around exactly these principles: fixed scope, published <a href="/lp/7-day-website/ph">peso pricing</a>, a clear 7-business-day timeline, and a domain, site and content that are yours. Compare it against any other quote you have.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'website-maintenance-guide',
      title: "Website Maintenance for Small Businesses: What It Costs and Why Skipping It Is Expensive",
      category: 'Development',
      date: '13 Aug 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Justin Sumaya',
      authorRole: 'Head of Development',
      authorPhoto: 'https://fazeddigital.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/151652330_3908937289163068_8338977159966182887_n-450x450.jpg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1461749280684-dccba630e2f6?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Outdated plugins, expired SSL and broken forms quietly cost small businesses leads every month. Here's what website maintenance involves and what it should cost.",
      content: `
        <p>Website maintenance means keeping your site updated, secure, backed up and working. Most small businesses should do it monthly, either themselves or through a paid plan. Costs vary widely with the platform and the support included, but skipping it usually costs more, because broken forms and hacked sites quietly lose you leads.</p>
        <p>Most small business websites are launched with enthusiasm and then left alone. For a while, nothing seems wrong. Then a contact form silently stops sending emails, a plugin update breaks the layout, or the site gets flagged as unsafe because the SSL certificate expired. By the time someone notices, weeks of leads may be gone. Maintenance is not glamorous, but it is cheap insurance.</p>
        <h2>What website maintenance actually includes</h2>
        <p>A proper maintenance routine covers five areas. <strong>Software updates</strong>: the CMS core, themes and plugins. <strong>Security</strong>: monitoring for malware, blocking brute-force login attempts and keeping SSL valid. <strong>Backups</strong>: automated, stored off-site and tested so they can actually be restored. <strong>Performance</strong>: checking load speed and fixing regressions. <strong>Functional checks</strong>: confirming that forms, checkout, booking tools and links still work.</p>
        <p>The backup point deserves emphasis. A backup you have never restored is only a hope. Do a test restore at least once a year, and keep a copy somewhere separate from your web host. If your only backup sits on the same server as the site, one failure can take out both.</p>
        <h2>Why WordPress sites need it most</h2>
        <p>WordPress powers a huge share of the web, which makes it a favourite target for automated attacks. The WordPress core is rarely the weak point. Patchstack, a WordPress security company, reports year after year that the large majority of newly found vulnerabilities are in plugins. A site with fifteen plugins, none updated in a year, is an open door. Monthly updates, tested on a staging copy first, close most of those doors.</p>
        <p>Static and modern framework sites, such as the ones we build with Astro, have a much smaller attack surface because no database or admin panel is exposed to the public. They still need dependency updates and monitoring, but the risk and the effort are lower. If you are still choosing a platform, this is one of the trade-offs covered in <a href="/blog/wordpress-vs-custom-build">WordPress vs custom build</a>.</p>
        <h2>The hidden costs of skipping maintenance</h2>
        <p><strong>Lost leads</strong> are the biggest cost and the hardest to see. A broken form does not announce itself. Enquiries simply stop. <strong>Cleanup after a hack</strong> typically costs far more than a year of maintenance, and if Google flags your site as dangerous, visitors will see a warning until the problem is fixed and reviewed. <strong>Emergency rebuilds</strong> happen when a site has been neglected for so long that updating it safely is no longer possible.</p>
        <p>There is one more quiet risk: an expired domain. If the renewal notice goes to an old email address and the domain lapses, your site and your email can both stop working. Someone else may even be able to buy it.</p>
        <h2>What maintenance should cost</h2>
        <p>Rates vary widely from one provider to another, so judge a plan by what it includes rather than the price alone. The cost depends on the platform, the number of plugins, how many content changes are included and how quickly someone responds when things go wrong. A simple static site costs less to look after than an online store with dozens of plugins.</p>
        <p>For reference, our own care plans start from ₱3,500 a month, or $99 a month outside the Philippines, and cover updates, backups, security monitoring, uptime checks and small content changes. That is our price, not a market average, so compare it with other quotes.</p>
        <p>Be wary of very cheap plans. They often mean automated updates with no testing, which can break your site just as easily as neglect.</p>
        <h2>Questions to ask before you sign up</h2>
        <p>Ask any provider six things. Do you test updates before applying them to the live site? How often do you back up, where are the backups stored and when was a restore last tested? What happens if the site gets hacked, and is cleanup included? How quickly do you respond? How many content changes are included? Will I receive a report each month? If you are also reviewing your hosting, our <a href="/blog/web-hosting-small-business-guide">web hosting guide</a> explains what to look for.</p>
        <h2>A monthly checklist you can follow yourself</h2>
        <p>If you manage your own site, block out one hour a month. Take a full backup. Update plugins and themes one at a time, checking the site after each. Submit every form on the site and confirm the email arrives. Test the site on your phone. Check Google Search Console for errors. Confirm your SSL certificate and domain are set to auto-renew, because certificate lifetimes keep getting shorter. Write down anything unusual.</p>
        <h2>When to hand it over</h2>
        <p>If your website generates real revenue, if you have more than a handful of plugins, or if you never get around to that monthly hour, it is time to delegate. The goal is a site that quietly works every day while you run the business.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>Can I skip maintenance on a small website?</strong> A small site still needs updates, backups and working forms. It simply takes less time. A static site needs less attention, but not none.</p>
        <p><strong>How often should I update WordPress plugins?</strong> Check at least monthly, and apply security updates as soon as you can after taking a backup.</p>
        <p>Prefer not to think about any of this? Every site launched through our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> can go onto an optional monthly care plan, so updates, backups and monitoring are looked after for you.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'brand-colours-guide',
      title: "How to Choose Brand Colours: A Practical Guide for Small Businesses",
      category: 'Branding',
      date: '06 Aug 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Lord Arcamo',
      authorRole: 'Founder',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-lord.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1581291518857-4d27a540d9f2?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Brand colours shape how customers feel before they read a word. Learn how to choose a colour palette that fits your market, stands out and works on screen.",
      content: `
        <p>To choose brand colours, start by studying your competitors and your market, then pick one primary colour, one or two accents and a set of neutrals. Test them on real screens and printed items, check that text is readable, and write down the exact values. Your favourite colour should not drive the decision.</p>
        <p>Colour sets expectations before anyone reads a headline: premium or affordable, playful or serious, traditional or modern. Yet most small businesses pick their colours based on personal preference, and end up looking exactly like their competitors. Here is a more deliberate way to choose.</p>
        <h2>Start with your market, not your favourite colour</h2>
        <p>Before opening a colour picker, list your five closest competitors and screenshot their websites and logos side by side. You will often see a pattern: every dental clinic in blue and white, every food brand in red and yellow, every eco brand in green. That pattern is useful information. You can choose to follow the category convention so customers instantly understand what you do, or break it deliberately to stand out. <strong>Both are valid. Doing it by accident is not.</strong></p>
        <p>How do you decide? Follow the convention when trust and familiarity matter most, such as in healthcare or finance. Break it when you need to be noticed in a crowded market and your personality is part of the offer. Either way, write down a short sentence describing how you want customers to feel, because it will help you make the choices that follow.</p>
        <h2>Understand what colours signal, loosely</h2>
        <p>Colour psychology is often overstated, and meanings vary across cultures, but some broad associations are reliable enough to use. Blues tend to signal trust and stability, which is why banks and tech companies love them. Greens suggest health, growth and nature. Reds and oranges create energy and urgency. Black and deep neutrals read as premium and confident. Bright, unexpected colours, like the electric lime we use at Fazed, signal boldness and a modern edge.</p>
        <p>Use these as starting points, not rules. Context, shade and combination matter more than the hue alone. A soft sage green and a neon green send very different messages, even though both are green. When we built the identity for <a href="/work/02">Cleen &amp; Green</a>, a premium cleaning company in the Philippines, a fresh green palette was chosen because it fitted their eco-conscious values, not because green is the default for cleaning.</p>
        <h2>Build a palette, not just a colour</h2>
        <p>A working brand palette usually has three layers. A <strong>primary colour</strong> that people will associate with you. One or two <strong>secondary or accent colours</strong> for highlights, buttons and calls to action. A set of <strong>neutrals</strong>, typically a near-black, a near-white and a few greys, which will actually make up most of your website and documents.</p>
        <p>A common rule of thumb is roughly 60% neutral, 30% primary and 10% accent. The accent is your most valuable colour: reserve it for the things you want people to click. If everything is bright, nothing stands out.</p>
        <p>Do not forget functional colours. Your website also needs a clear colour for error messages and success messages, and these need to work with your palette. Pure black and pure white can feel harsh on screen, so many brands use a very dark grey and a slightly warm white instead.</p>
        <h2>A simple way to build yours in an afternoon</h2>
        <p>Here is a process any owner can follow. First, write three words for how the brand should feel, such as "calm, expert, local". Second, take your competitor screenshots and note which colours are overused. Third, pick one primary colour that fits your three words and is different enough from your nearest rivals. Fourth, add a contrasting accent, then a dark and a light neutral. Fifth, mock up a simple page with a headline, a paragraph and a button, and see if you can read it easily. Free tools such as Adobe Color and Coolors make it easy to try combinations.</p>
        <h2>Test it where it will actually live</h2>
        <p>A palette that looks beautiful in a design file can fail in the real world. Test your colours on a phone screen in daylight, printed on a business card, embroidered on a uniform and as a tiny social media profile picture.</p>
        <p>Check text contrast against accessibility guidelines too. The Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) ask for a ratio of at least 4.5:1 for normal text, and a free contrast checker will test your colours in seconds. Low-contrast grey text on a white background is one of the most common reasons websites feel hard to read. Do not rely on colour alone to carry meaning either, because some of your customers will be colour-blind.</p>
        <h2>Document it and stay consistent</h2>
        <p>Once chosen, write down the exact values: HEX and RGB for screens, CMYK for print, and Pantone if you produce merchandise. Colours on screen and on paper never match exactly, so ask your printer for a proof before a big run. Share the values with everyone who creates anything for your brand, ideally in a one-page <a href="/blog/brand-style-guide">brand style guide</a>.</p>
        <p>Consistency is what turns a colour into recognition. A brand that uses three slightly different shades of blue across its signage, website and Facebook page looks less careful, even if customers cannot say why.</p>
        <h2>When to revisit your colours</h2>
        <p>If your colours are hard to read, clash with your photography, or make you indistinguishable from competitors, it is worth refreshing them. Often a modest adjustment, a deeper shade or a sharper accent, is enough without a full rebrand.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>How many colours should a brand have?</strong> Most small businesses do well with one primary colour, one or two accents and a few neutrals. More than that gets hard to keep consistent.</p>
        <p><strong>Can I use the same colour as a competitor?</strong> You can, as colour alone rarely identifies a business, but you will have to work harder to stand apart. Try a different shade or accent if they are in your area.</p>
        <p>Your website is where your palette works hardest. In our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a>, you share your logo and colour references on Day 1, and you see them working across your design mockups by Day 2 to 3.</p>
      `,
