    {
      slug: 'homepage-copywriting-that-sells',
      title: "Homepage Copywriting: How to Write Website Copy That Turns Visitors Into Customers",
      category: 'Strategy',
      date: '30 Jul 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Reancirl Balaba',
      authorRole: 'Co-Founder',
      authorPhoto: 'https://fazeddigital.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/117749302_4540601115965165_6039017566881807039_n-450x450.jpg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1434626881859-194d67b2b86f?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Most homepages talk about the business instead of the customer. Here's a simple, proven structure for homepage copy that makes visitors understand, trust and act.",
      content: `
        <p>Good homepage copywriting says what you do, who it is for and why it matters, in words your customer would use. Write a clear headline, name the customer's problem, show the benefit, prove it and give one next step. You have a few seconds to convince a visitor they are in the right place.</p>
        <p>Most small business homepages waste those seconds with headlines such as "Welcome to Our Website" or "Excellence in Every Detail". These say nothing about what the business does or why anyone should care. Good homepage copy is not clever. It is clear, specific and built around the customer's problem.</p>
        <h2>The headline: say what you do and who it is for</h2>
        <p>Your headline has one job: tell visitors exactly what you offer, in words they would use themselves. A useful test is whether a stranger could repeat back what you do after reading it once. "Aircon cleaning and repair for homes and offices in Iligan City" beats "Your Comfort, Our Commitment" every time. Underneath, a short subheadline can add the key benefit or differentiator: same-day service, fixed pricing or a long warranty, as long as you really offer it.</p>
        <h2>Lead with the customer's problem</h2>
        <p>People do not buy services. They buy solutions to problems. Early on the page, name the problem your customer has in their own language. "Tired of contractors who never show up on time?" or "Spending hours every week answering the same customer questions?" When visitors see their own frustration described accurately, they keep reading because they feel understood.</p>
        <p>You do not have to guess at this language. Read your reviews, your Messenger chats and your enquiry emails. Copy the phrases customers use when they describe the problem, then use those words on your page. A customer's own sentence will almost always beat anything you write in a meeting.</p>
        <h2>Write benefits, then back them with features</h2>
        <p>A feature is what something is. A benefit is what it does for the customer. "Cloud-based booking system" is a feature. "Customers book appointments at 11pm without calling you" is a benefit. Lead with the benefit, then mention the feature that makes it possible. <strong>Every sentence on your homepage should answer the visitor's silent question: what is in it for me?</strong></p>
        <p>A quick way to do this is the "so what?" test. Take each feature on your page and ask "so what?" until you reach something a customer would care about. "We use hospital-grade products." So what? "Your home is safe for kids and pets the moment we leave." That second line is your copy.</p>
        <h2>Prove it with specifics</h2>
        <p>Claims are cheap. Evidence persuades. Replace vague superlatives with concrete proof. Instead of "trusted by many clients", give the real number of clients and the real year you started. Add two or three short testimonials with real names and, ideally, photos, following our guide to <a href="/blog/client-testimonials-strategy">getting testimonials clients are happy to give</a>. Show the logos of clients or certifications you have permission to use. Mention numbers wherever they are honest: years in business, projects completed or average response time. If a number is not true or you cannot back it up, leave it out.</p>
        <h2>Make the next step obvious</h2>
        <p>Every homepage needs one primary call to action, repeated at natural points down the page. Make it specific and low-pressure: "Get a free quote in 24 hours" works better than "Submit". Avoid offering five different actions of equal weight, because too many choices often lead to no choice at all. If you have a secondary action, such as browsing your work, style it less prominently.</p>
        <h2>A before and after</h2>
        <p>Here is a made-up example for a bookkeeping firm. Before: "Welcome to ABC Solutions. We are a dynamic team committed to delivering excellence in financial services." It could describe almost anyone, and it gives the reader nothing to do.</p>
        <p>After: "Bookkeeping and BIR filing for small businesses in Cagayan de Oro. Stop worrying about deadlines. We keep your books up to date and file on time, for a fixed monthly fee. Get a quote in one business day." It names the service, the customer, the place, the benefit, the pricing model and the next step, all in about the same space.</p>
        <h2>Cut ruthlessly</h2>
        <p>Once you have a draft, remove every sentence that does not help a visitor understand, trust or act. Kill the corporate filler: "solutions", "world-class", "one-stop shop". Use short paragraphs and plain words. Read it aloud. If you would not say it to a customer face to face, rewrite it. Our guide to <a href="/blog/brand-voice-guide">finding your brand voice</a> will help you sound like yourself.</p>
        <h2>A simple homepage structure</h2>
        <p>Headline and subheadline. Primary call to action. The problem. Your solution and its key benefits. Proof: testimonials, numbers, logos. How it works in three steps. A final call to action. This structure is not fancy, but it suits almost every industry, and it gives you a clear order to write in.</p>
        <h2>Test it before you publish</h2>
        <p>Show your homepage to someone who does not know your business for five seconds, then hide it. Ask what you do, who it is for and what they would do next. If they cannot answer all three, the page is not clear yet. Try this with two or three people on a phone, because that is where most people will read it.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>How much text should a homepage have?</strong> Enough to explain what you do and why to trust you, then direct people to the right page. Short sections work better than long blocks.</p>
        <p><strong>Should I write my homepage copy myself?</strong> You can. You know your customers best. Start with this structure, then ask a customer or a copywriter to check it for clarity.</p>
        <p>Copy and design work best when built together. In our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a>, you review your design mockups on Day 2 to 3, so you can see your message and layout working together before anything is built.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'on-page-seo-checklist',
      title: "On-Page SEO Checklist: How to Optimise Every Page of Your Small Business Website",
      category: 'SEO',
      date: '23 Jul 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Russel Heyrana',
      authorRole: 'Marketing Director',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-russel.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1517180102446-f3ece451e9d8?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Titles, headings, URLs, internal links and images: a practical on-page SEO checklist you can apply to every page today, no technical background needed.",
      content: `
        <p>On-page SEO is the part of search optimisation you fully control: the keyword focus, title, headings, URL, content, links and images on each page. Run each important page through this eight-point checklist. You cannot force other sites to link to you, but you can make every page clear to both search engines and people.</p>
        <p>You do not need a technical background to do this. Start with your homepage and your top three service pages, because those usually matter most to your business. Allow about 30 minutes a page, and keep a simple spreadsheet of what you have checked.</p>
        <h2>1. One primary keyword per page</h2>
        <p>Every page should target one main search phrase, plus a few close variations. Your "Services" page cannot rank for ten different services at once, so give each important service its own page. Before writing, search the phrase on Google yourself. If the results are all articles, a sales page will struggle. If they are all service pages, an article will struggle. <strong>Match the format Google is already rewarding.</strong></p>
        <p>If you are not sure which phrases to use, our guide to <a href="/blog/keyword-research-small-business">keyword research for small businesses</a> shows you how to find them for free. Make sure two pages are not chasing the same phrase, because they will compete with each other.</p>
        <h2>2. Title tag and meta description</h2>
        <p>The title tag is the blue link in search results and one of the strongest on-page signals. Keep it under about 60 characters, put the primary keyword near the start and include your brand at the end. For example: "Commercial Cleaning Services in Davao | CleanPro". Every page needs its own title. A site with the same title on every page wastes one of its easiest opportunities.</p>
        <p>The meta description does not directly affect rankings, but it affects whether people click. Write 140 to 160 characters that make a specific promise and include the keyword naturally. Google may sometimes rewrite it, and that is normal.</p>
        <h2>3. A single, clear H1 and logical headings</h2>
        <p>Each page needs one H1 heading that states the topic, usually close to your title tag. Use H2s for main sections and H3s for subsections, in order, like a table of contents. Headings help Google understand structure and help readers scan. Many sites use heading tags purely for visual styling, which muddies the signal. If you only want bigger text, change the style, not the tag.</p>
        <h2>4. Clean, descriptive URLs</h2>
        <p>A good URL is short, lowercase, uses hyphens and describes the page: /services/office-cleaning rather than /page?id=4372. Once a page is live and ranking, avoid changing its URL. If you must, set up a 301 redirect from the old address so you keep the authority it has earned, and update your internal links to the new one.</p>
        <h2>5. Content that fully answers the search</h2>
        <p>Think about what someone searching your keyword actually wants to know: prices, process, timelines, locations served, common questions. Answer them clearly on the page. Include the keyword in the first paragraph and use related terms naturally throughout, but never stuff it. If the page reads awkwardly, it is over-optimised.</p>
        <p>There is no ideal word count. A contact page can be short, while a service page usually needs enough detail to help someone decide. Look at the top results for your keyword and ask what they cover that you do not. Then add what they miss, such as your real process, your own photos or the questions your customers ask you. That first-hand detail is what sets your page apart.</p>
        <h2>6. Internal links</h2>
        <p>Link related pages together using descriptive anchor text: "see our office cleaning packages" rather than "click here". Internal links help Google discover pages, understand how they relate and pass authority from strong pages, such as your homepage and popular articles, to the pages that generate revenue. Every blog post should link to at least one relevant service page. Check for orphan pages too: if nothing links to a page, both visitors and Google will struggle to find it.</p>
        <h2>7. Images that help instead of hurt</h2>
        <p>Compress images before uploading, because large photos are a very common cause of slow pages. Use modern formats such as WebP where possible. Give every meaningful image descriptive alt text, which helps accessibility and image search. Describe what is in the picture, and do not cram keywords in. Name files descriptively too: "office-cleaning-team.webp", not "IMG_4821.jpg".</p>
        <h2>8. Mobile and speed</h2>
        <p>Google primarily uses the mobile version of your site. Open every key page on your phone and check that text is readable without zooming, buttons are easy to tap and nothing overflows the screen. Test speed with PageSpeed Insights and fix the biggest issues it flags.</p>
        <h2>A worked example</h2>
        <p>Imagine a company in Davao that offers office cleaning. Today its services are all listed on one page titled "Services | CleanPro". After this checklist, the business has a dedicated page at /services/office-cleaning. The title is "Office Cleaning in Davao | CleanPro". The H1 is "Office Cleaning Services in Davao". The page explains what is included, how pricing works, which areas are covered and what a first visit looks like. It has a short FAQ and real photos of the team at work, and it links to a related page on deep cleaning. Nothing clever has happened. The page is simply clear about what it offers, and that is most of on-page SEO.</p>
        <h2>How to check your progress</h2>
        <p>Give changes a few weeks to take effect. In Google Search Console, open the Performance report, filter by page and compare impressions, clicks and average position before and after. Do not change everything at once if you can avoid it, or you will not know what helped. For problems that sit below the page level, such as crawling and indexing, read our guide to <a href="/blog/technical-seo-small-business">technical SEO</a>.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>How long does on-page SEO take to work?</strong> Google needs to recrawl your pages first, which can take days or weeks. Ranking changes often take longer, especially in competitive markets.</p>
        <p><strong>Do I need an SEO plugin?</strong> Not always. On WordPress, a plugin makes it easier to edit titles and descriptions. It only gives you the tools, though. You still have to do the work.</p>
        <p>Applying this checklist to an old site can take days. Every site built through our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> ships with on-page SEO setup included, so the basics are in place from launch.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'website-speed-fixes',
      title: "Why Is My Website So Slow? 7 Common Causes and How to Fix Them",
      category: 'Development',
      date: '16 Jul 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Justin Sumaya',
      authorRole: 'Head of Development',
      authorPhoto: 'https://fazeddigital.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/151652330_3908937289163068_8338977159966182887_n-450x450.jpg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1498050108023-c5249f4df085?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "A slow website loses visitors, rankings and sales. Here are the seven most common reasons small business sites load slowly, and the practical fix for each one.",
      content: `
        <p>Your website is probably slow because of oversized images, too many plugins, cheap hosting, heavy themes, third-party scripts, missing caching or badly loaded fonts. Start with images, as they are usually the biggest and easiest fix. Then test your pages with Google PageSpeed Insights, and work through the remaining causes in order of impact.</p>
        <p>Speed is one of the few website problems that hurts you three ways at once. Visitors leave before the page loads, page experience feeds into Google's ranking systems, and slow checkout or booking flows lose sales directly. On mobile data, every extra second matters even more. Here are the seven causes to check, and what to do about each one.</p>
        <h2>Before you fix anything, measure</h2>
        <p>Run your homepage and your most important service page through PageSpeed Insights, and write down the mobile results. Test the same pages again after each change. Without a starting point, you cannot tell what helped, and you may waste money on a fix that made no difference. Take a full backup before you start, too.</p>
        <h2>1. Oversized images</h2>
        <p>This is the number one culprit. A photo straight from a phone or camera can be several megabytes. Upload ten of those on a homepage and you have a very heavy page. <strong>The fix:</strong> resize images to the largest size they will actually display, compress them and serve modern formats such as WebP or AVIF. Lazy-load images below the fold so they only download when needed, but do not lazy-load the main image at the top of the page. Our <a href="/blog/image-optimisation-small-business">image optimisation guide</a> covers this step by step.</p>
        <h2>2. Too many plugins</h2>
        <p>On WordPress especially, a plugin can add scripts, styles and database queries to every page, even pages that do not use it. Over time, sites collect plugins that are inactive or that do the same job as another one. <strong>The fix:</strong> audit your plugins, remove anything unused, and replace heavy multi-purpose plugins with lighter, single-purpose alternatives or simple custom code. It is the quality of a plugin that counts more than the number, so test the site after removing each one.</p>
        <h2>3. Cheap or overcrowded hosting</h2>
        <p>Very cheap shared hosting puts many sites on one server. When a neighbour gets a traffic spike, your site can slow down. Servers located far from your customers also add delay. <strong>The fix:</strong> choose hosting with good performance reviews and servers in or near your market, or move to a modern static hosting platform with a global CDN. A slow "Time to First Byte" in your test results is a strong hint that the server is the issue. Our <a href="/blog/web-hosting-small-business-guide">web hosting guide</a> explains your options.</p>
        <h2>4. Heavy page builders and themes</h2>
        <p>Drag-and-drop builders and do-everything themes are convenient, but they often load large amounts of code to support features you never use. <strong>The fix:</strong> if speed is critical, consider a leaner theme or a modern framework build. Sites built with tools such as Astro ship little or no JavaScript by default, which gives them a strong head start on PageSpeed tests.</p>
        <h2>5. Third-party scripts</h2>
        <p>Chat widgets, social feeds, multiple analytics tools, ad pixels and embedded videos each load code from other servers you do not control. Individually they seem small, but together they can add seconds. <strong>The fix:</strong> list every third-party script, remove what you do not actively use, and load the rest after the main content, or only when a visitor interacts. For YouTube videos, show a thumbnail image that loads the player only when someone clicks it.</p>
        <h2>6. No caching</h2>
        <p>Without caching, your server rebuilds every page from scratch for every visitor. <strong>The fix:</strong> enable page caching on your server or via a plugin, set proper browser caching headers so returning visitors do not re-download unchanged files, and put a CDN in front of the site. Remember to clear the cache after you make changes, or you may see an old version and think your edit failed.</p>
        <h2>7. Render-blocking fonts and CSS</h2>
        <p>Custom fonts and large stylesheets loaded in the wrong way force the browser to wait before showing anything. <strong>The fix:</strong> limit the number of font weights, host fonts on your own domain where you can, use font-display so text shows straight away, and inline the small amount of CSS needed for the first screen.</p>
        <h2>Which fixes to do yourself, and which to hand over</h2>
        <p>You can usually handle the first few without a developer. Compressing images, removing plugins, deleting scripts you no longer need and turning on a caching plugin are all within reach of a careful owner. Take a backup first, and change one thing at a time.</p>
        <p>Moving hosts, rebuilding a theme, and changing how fonts and CSS load are better left to a developer, because mistakes can break the layout. If you have tried the simple fixes and your mobile results are still poor, the problem is probably in the foundations of the site. That is when a rebuild can be cheaper than months of patching.</p>
        <h2>How to measure your progress</h2>
        <p>Focus on mobile scores and the <a href="/blog/core-web-vitals-seo">Core Web Vitals</a>. Largest Contentful Paint under 2.5 seconds is the target to aim for. Fix the biggest opportunities first and re-test after each change. Do not obsess over a perfect 100. Scores vary a little between runs, and a page that feels fast on a real phone matters more than the last few points.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>What is a good load time for a small business website?</strong> Aim for your main content to appear within 2.5 seconds on a phone, which is Google's target for Largest Contentful Paint.</p>
        <p><strong>Why is my site fast for me and slow for customers?</strong> You probably have a quick connection, a good device and a cached copy of the site. Test on a mid-range phone using mobile data to see what your customers see.</p>
        <p>Sometimes the fastest fix is a rebuild. Sites from our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> are built lightweight and mobile-first, and optimised to load in under 2 seconds.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'facebook-page-vs-website',
      title: "Facebook Page vs Website: Why Philippine Businesses Need Both",
      category: 'Strategy',
      date: '09 Jul 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Reancirl Balaba',
      authorRole: 'Co-Founder',
      authorPhoto: 'https://fazeddigital.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/117749302_4540601115965165_6039017566881807039_n-450x450.jpg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1524781289445-ddf8f5695861?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Many Philippine businesses run entirely on Facebook. Here's why that's risky, what a website does that Facebook can't, and how to make both work together.",
      content: `
        <p>Philippine businesses need both a Facebook page and a website, because each does a different job. Facebook helps people discover you and message you. A website gives you something you control, shows up on Google and earns the trust of bigger buyers. Relying on Facebook alone is a risk most owners do not see until it hurts.</p>
        <p>In the Philippines, Facebook is where business happens. Customers discover shops through their feed, ask questions over Messenger and place orders in the comments. So it is a fair question to ask whether you need a website if your page is doing well. The honest answer is yes, and not because a website replaces Facebook.</p>
        <h2>You do not own your Facebook page</h2>
        <p>Your page, your followers and your content live on a platform whose rules can change overnight. Pages get restricted for reasons that are hard to appeal. Accounts get hacked. Organic reach has declined for years, so your posts may reach only a small share of your followers unless you pay to boost them.</p>
        <p><strong>A website is the online asset you control most.</strong> You own the domain and the content, and if you are unhappy with your host, you can move. No algorithm decides how many people get to see your homepage.</p>
        <p>Think about what would happen if your page disappeared tomorrow. Would you still have your customers' contact details? Would they know where to find you? If the answer is no, you are one locked account away from starting again.</p>
        <h2>Google does not show Facebook posts for most searches</h2>
        <p>When someone searches "catering services in Cagayan de Oro" or "affordable dentist near me", Google mostly shows websites and Google Business Profiles, not Facebook posts. Customers who search, rather than scroll, are often the most ready to buy. Without a website, you can be invisible at the exact moment of highest intent. A complete <a href="/blog/google-business-profile-optimization">Google Business Profile</a> linked to your website helps close that gap.</p>
        <h2>Websites build trust that Facebook cannot</h2>
        <p>Corporate clients, overseas customers and larger buyers often check for a proper website before contacting a business. A professional site with your own domain and email address, such as hello@yourbusiness.com instead of a Gmail account, signals that you are established and serious. For B2B services especially, having no website can quietly cost you deals you never knew about.</p>
        <h2>What Facebook does better</h2>
        <p>To be fair, Facebook is excellent at things websites are not: reaching people who were not searching for you, building community, sharing quick updates and handling fast conversations over Messenger. The goal is not to choose one. It is to give each a clear job.</p>
        <h2>How to make them work together</h2>
        <p>Use <strong>Facebook for discovery and conversation</strong>, and your <strong>website as the home base</strong> where customers get the full picture and take action. In practice, put your website link in your page bio, post buttons and ad destinations. Use your website for the things Facebook handles poorly, such as detailed service pages, pricing, portfolios, booking forms, FAQs and blog content that ranks on Google. Add a "Message us" button to your website that opens Messenger using your page's m.me link, so visitors can still contact you the way they prefer. Meta retired its old on-site chat plugin in 2024, so older guides that recommend it are out of date.</p>
        <p>Install the Meta Pixel on your website so you can show Facebook ads to people who have already visited. Someone who browsed your pricing page but did not enquire is a far warmer audience than a stranger. Mention this tracking in your privacy policy.</p>
        <h2>What the split looks like</h2>
        <p>Picture a caterer in Cagayan de Oro. On Facebook, they post photos from last weekend's events and answer questions on Messenger. Their website holds the sample menus, the price guide, the service area and a booking enquiry form. A couple planning a wedding finds them through Google, sees their menus, then checks the Facebook page to see recent work. When a post gets attention, the link in the comments sends people to the website. Each channel does what it does best.</p>
        <h2>Protect your page while you are at it</h2>
        <p>Keep using Facebook, but reduce the risk. Turn on two-factor authentication. Add a second trusted admin, so a single hacked or lost account does not lock you out. Save your best photos and customer contact details somewhere outside Facebook. Ask regular customers for their email address or mobile number, with their permission, so you can still reach them if your page ever goes down.</p>
        <h2>What a small business website really needs</h2>
        <p>You do not need a huge site. A clear homepage, a page for each main service, an about page with real photos, a contact page with a form and map, and a way to show your reviews will put you ahead of competitors who rely on Facebook alone. If you sell online, you can also <a href="/blog/payment-gateway-philippines-gcash">accept GCash and other local payments</a> on your site.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>Can I just use Facebook if my business is very small?</strong> You can start there, and plenty of businesses do. Add a website once customers are searching for you or larger buyers need to check you out.</p>
        <p><strong>Will a website take away my Facebook customers?</strong> No. They do different jobs. Many customers will use both, and your website gives your Facebook followers more detail before they buy.</p>
        <p><strong>Can you use my Facebook content for the website?</strong> Yes. If you have a page, share it with us and we will pull your photos and details from it.</p>
        <p>Our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a>, with <a href="/lp/7-day-website/ph">Philippine pricing from ₱19,999</a>, is built for exactly this: a professional home base for your business, live in 7 business days if your content arrives on Day 1 and your feedback within 24 hours at each review.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'signs-website-needs-redesign',
      title: "7 Signs Your Business Website Needs a Redesign",
      category: 'Design',
      date: '02 Jul 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Lord Arcamo',
      authorRole: 'Founder',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-lord.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1550439062-609e1531270e?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Is your website quietly costing you customers? From slow load times to embarrassing mobile layouts, here are seven clear signs it's time for a redesign.",
      content: `
        <p>Your business website needs a redesign when it fails on phones, loads slowly, embarrasses you, no longer describes your business, brings in no enquiries, cannot be updated or runs on outdated technology. One or two small signs may only need a refresh. Three or more usually mean it is time for a rebuild.</p>
        <p>Websites age faster than most business owners realise. Technology improves, customer expectations rise and your business itself changes. A site that felt modern four years ago can now be turning customers away. Here are seven signs to check.</p>
        <h2>1. It looks broken or awkward on a phone</h2>
        <p>Open your site on your phone right now. If you have to pinch to zoom, if buttons are too small to tap, if text runs off the screen or menus do not work properly, you have a serious problem. Many of your visitors are on mobile, and Google primarily looks at the mobile version of your site. <strong>If you only fix one thing, fix this.</strong> Our guide to <a href="/blog/mobile-first-web-design">mobile-first web design</a> explains what good looks like.</p>
        <h2>2. It takes more than three seconds to load</h2>
        <p>Run your homepage through Google PageSpeed Insights. If your mobile score is in the red, visitors may leave before they ever see your offer. Google's own target is for the main content to load within 2.5 seconds. Older sites built on heavy themes and loaded with plugins often cannot be made fast without rebuilding the foundations.</p>
        <h2>3. You are embarrassed to share the link</h2>
        <p>This sounds unscientific, but it is a very useful signal. If you hesitate before giving your website to a potential client, or you tell people "just check our Facebook instead", your website no longer represents the quality of your business. Your customers can see that gap too.</p>
        <h2>4. It no longer reflects what you actually do</h2>
        <p>Businesses evolve. You may have added services, dropped others, moved upmarket or changed your target customer. If your website still describes the business you were three years ago, it attracts the wrong enquiries and confuses the right ones. Small edits rarely fix this. The structure itself usually needs rethinking.</p>
        <h2>5. It is not generating enquiries</h2>
        <p>A website that gets traffic but no calls, form submissions or bookings has a conversion problem. Common causes include unclear messaging, no obvious call to action, forms that are too long, missing trust signals such as reviews, and pages that do not answer the questions customers actually have.</p>
        <p>Check the simple things first. Submit your own form and see whether the email arrives. A broken form can look exactly like a quiet month. If everything works and enquiries are still low, a redesign built around one clear action per page is a sensible next step.</p>
        <h2>6. You cannot update it yourself</h2>
        <p>If changing a price, adding a photo or posting an announcement means emailing a developer and waiting a week, your site is working against you. A modern site should let you edit everyday content easily, or come with a support arrangement that makes updates quick.</p>
        <h2>7. It is built on outdated or insecure technology</h2>
        <p>Sites running on old PHP versions, abandoned themes or plugins that are no longer maintained are security risks. Browser warnings about insecure connections and missing SSL certificates are the visible signs. Invisible ones, such as unpatched vulnerabilities, are more dangerous. If your developer has gone and nobody can log in or update the site, treat that as urgent.</p>
        <h2>Redesign or refresh?</h2>
        <p>If you recognised one or two minor signs, a refresh may be enough: new imagery, tighter copy, a faster host. If you recognised three or more, especially mobile, speed or conversion problems, a full redesign is usually better value than continued patching. Each patch costs money and still leaves you with the same old foundations.</p>
        <p>The exception is a site that performs well. If it already brings in steady enquiries and ranks for searches you care about, do not throw that away because you are tired of the look. Improve it in stages instead.</p>
        <h2>What to do before you redesign</h2>
        <p>A redesign can damage what is already working if you rush it, so do four things first. List every page on your current site, and use Search Console and analytics to see which ones bring in visitors. Save your analytics history and confirm you have access to your domain and hosting. Decide what the new site must achieve, such as more quote requests, and write that down.</p>
        <p>Then plan your redirects. If any URL changes, point the old address to the new one with a 301 redirect, or you risk losing rankings you already have. Ask any designer you hire how they handle this. If they have no answer, keep looking.</p>
        <p>Once the new site is live, keep it healthy with a simple <a href="/blog/website-maintenance-guide">maintenance routine</a>, so you are not in the same position a few years from now.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>How often should a business redesign its website?</strong> There is no fixed schedule. Many businesses find they need one every few years, but the signs above matter more than the calendar.</p>
        <p><strong>Will a redesign hurt my Google rankings?</strong> It can if pages disappear or URLs change without redirects. With a careful plan, your rankings should hold up, and a faster, clearer site gives you a better base.</p>
        <p>Redesigns do not need to take months. Our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> rebuilds your site mobile-first and fast-loading in 7 business days, as long as your content arrives on Day 1 and your feedback comes within 24 hours at each review.</p>
      `,
    },
