      excerpt: "Your Google Business Profile can bring in more calls than your website. Follow these 10 practical steps to optimise it and climb into Google's local Map Pack.",
      content: `
        <p>To optimise your Google Business Profile, claim and verify it, choose the right primary category, keep your business details accurate, complete every section, add real photos and earn reviews steadily. Google says local results depend on relevance, distance and prominence. A complete, active profile improves the two you can influence, and it is free.</p>
        <p>When someone searches for a service "near me" or in a specific city, Google usually shows a map with a few businesses before the regular results. That box, the local Map Pack, is one of the most valuable spots a local business can hold. You cannot pay your way into the free listings, as Google says there is no way to request or buy a better local ranking. Here are ten steps to do it properly.</p>
        <h2>1. Claim and verify your profile</h2>
        <p>Search for your business on Google Maps. If a profile exists, claim it. If not, create one at business.google.com. Complete the verification process, which may involve a video, a phone call or a postcard, depending on what Google offers you. Until you verify, you cannot properly manage how your business appears, so make this your first job. Use an email address the business owns, not one belonging to a staff member or agency.</p>
        <h2>2. Choose the right primary category</h2>
        <p>Your primary category is widely seen as one of the strongest local ranking factors. Be specific: "Wedding Photographer" rather than "Photographer", "Italian Restaurant" rather than "Restaurant". Add relevant secondary categories for other services you offer, but do not add categories that do not genuinely apply. To see what is working, search your main service and check which categories the top businesses use.</p>
        <h2>3. Get your name, address and phone exactly right</h2>
        <p>Use your real business name, with no keyword stuffing. Google can suspend profiles that add phrases like "Best Dentist Iligan" to their name. Make sure your address and phone number match exactly what appears on your website, Facebook page and directory listings. <strong>Consistency across the web builds Google's confidence in your business.</strong></p>
        <p>If you travel to customers and do not serve them at your address, set yourself up as a service-area business, list the areas you cover and hide your address. Do not use a virtual office or a P.O. box to appear in another city, as this breaks Google's guidelines.</p>
        <h2>4. Write a useful business description</h2>
        <p>You have 750 characters. Describe what you do, who you serve, where you operate and what makes you different. Include your main service and location naturally. Avoid links, promotions and all-caps text. Put the most important information first, because only the beginning may be visible at a glance.</p>
        <h2>5. Add services and products</h2>
        <p>List each service individually with a short description and, where possible, a price or price range. This gives Google more context for matching your profile to specific searches and helps customers decide before they call. Use the words your customers use. A clinic might list "teeth whitening" and "dental cleaning" rather than only "cosmetic dentistry".</p>
        <h2>6. Upload real, high-quality photos regularly</h2>
        <p>Add your logo, a cover photo, exterior shots so people can find you, interior shots, your team and examples of your work. Genuine photos help customers trust you and recognise your location. Keep adding new ones every month to show you are active. Skip stock images, because customers can spot them and they tell people nothing about your business.</p>
        <h2>7. Set accurate hours, including holidays</h2>
        <p>Few things frustrate a customer more than arriving to find a business closed when Google said it was open. Keep regular hours accurate and set special hours for holidays such as Christmas, New Year and Holy Week.</p>
        <h2>8. Build a steady stream of reviews</h2>
        <p>Google says that more reviews and positive ratings can help your local ranking, and they influence customers too. Ask every satisfied customer for a review, ideally with a direct link sent by SMS or Messenger right after a successful job. Aim for a steady flow rather than a sudden burst. Our guide to <a href="/blog/get-more-google-reviews">getting more Google reviews</a> includes wording you can use.</p>
        <p><strong>Respond to every review</strong>, positive and negative, politely and professionally. Never buy fake reviews or offer rewards for good ones. Google removes them and can penalise your profile.</p>
        <h2>9. Post updates weekly</h2>
        <p>Google Posts let you share offers, news, events and new work directly on your profile. They keep your listing fresh and give searchers more reasons to choose you. A short weekly post with a photo is enough. If weekly is too much, aim for a realistic schedule, such as twice a month, and keep it up.</p>
        <h2>10. Connect it to a strong website</h2>
        <p>Your profile links to your website, which gives Google more to work with when it judges what you do and where you do it. A fast, mobile-friendly site with a page for each service and your location clearly stated supports your profile. Link to the most relevant page, not always your homepage. If you have several branches, link each profile to its own location page.</p>
        <p>Your website also carries answers that used to sit elsewhere. Google has been phasing out the public Q&amp;A section on Business Profiles in favour of AI answers drawn from sources such as your profile, reviews and website. So put the answers to common customer questions on your site and in your profile, where you control the wording. For the wider picture, read our <a href="/blog/local-seo-small-business-guide">local SEO guide</a>.</p>
        <h2>Track what is working</h2>
        <p>Your profile's Performance section shows how people found you and what they did, including calls, website clicks and direction requests. Check it once a month and note what changed after you made updates. Tag the website link in your profile too, so you can see that traffic separately in your analytics. Then ask new customers how they found you, because the best data is often a direct answer.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>How long does it take to rank in the Map Pack?</strong> There is no fixed timeline. It depends on your competition, your distance from the searcher and the strength of your profile. Treat it as steady, ongoing work.</p>
        <p><strong>Can I have a Google Business Profile without an address?</strong> Yes. Service-area businesses can hide their address and list the areas they serve, but you still need to verify the business.</p>
        <p>A Google Business Profile works best with a website that backs it up. Our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> gives you a fast, mobile-first site with on-page SEO setup, so visitors from the Map Pack have somewhere clear to land and enquire.</p>
      `,
