    {
      slug: 'mobile-first-web-design',
      title: "Mobile-First Web Design: Why Your Website Must Be Built for Phones First",
      category: 'Design',
      date: '18 Jun 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Lord Arcamo',
      authorRole: 'Founder',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-lord.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1497091071254-cc9b2ba7c48a?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Most of your customers will see your website on a phone. Here's what mobile-first design really means, why Google cares, and how to get it right for your business.",
      content: `
        <p>Mobile-first web design means planning and building your website for a phone screen first, then adapting it for larger screens. It matters because many of your customers will see your site on a phone, and Google primarily uses the mobile version of your site to decide how to rank it.</p>
        <p>Yet many websites are still designed on a large desktop monitor and then squeezed down to fit a phone as an afterthought. The result is cramped layouts, tiny buttons and slow pages. Mobile-first design flips the process. Check your own analytics to see how much of your traffic is mobile. For many small businesses, and especially in the Philippines, it is the larger share.</p>
        <h2>What mobile-first actually means</h2>
        <p>Responsive design means a site adapts to different screen sizes. Mobile-first goes further: you <strong>design and build for the smallest screen first</strong>, deciding what truly matters, then progressively enhance the layout for tablets and desktops. Starting small forces clarity. When you only have a narrow column to work with, you have to decide what your visitor needs most and cut everything else.</p>
        <p>In practice, that changes the order of the work. Start with a list of what a phone visitor came to do, such as call you, check a price or find your address. Put those at the top. Then add the extras when the screen has room for them. A desktop-first process works the other way round, and the things that matter most often end up buried.</p>
        <h2>Google ranks the mobile version of your site</h2>
        <p>Google uses mobile-first indexing, which means it primarily looks at the mobile version of your website when deciding how to rank it. If content, headings or structured data are missing on mobile, your rankings can suffer on every device, including desktop.</p>
        <p>A common trap is hiding content on phones to make a page look tidier. If a section appears only on desktop, Google may not count it. Keep the same important content on both versions. Use accordions or tabs on small screens if you need to save space, rather than removing the content.</p>
        <h2>Design for thumbs, not cursors</h2>
        <p>On a phone, people navigate with their thumb, often one-handed. That changes everything. Buttons should be large enough to tap comfortably. Apple's design guidelines recommend at least 44 points, so treat 44 pixels as a sensible minimum for main buttons, with space between them. Place your primary call to action where thumbs naturally reach.</p>
        <p>Make phone numbers tappable to call and addresses tappable to open in maps. Avoid using hover effects as the only way to reveal important information, because there is no hover on a touchscreen. Many local businesses also benefit from a slim bar fixed to the bottom of the screen, with one Call or Enquire button, so the main action is never more than a tap away.</p>
        <h2>Prioritise speed on mobile data</h2>
        <p>Mobile visitors are often on slower or patchy connections. Every heavy image, video background and script costs more on mobile than on office Wi-Fi. Mobile-first design means compressing images aggressively, avoiding autoplay videos, loading only what the first screen needs and deferring everything else. A fast, plain page will usually serve your customers better than a prettier one that keeps them waiting. Our guide to <a href="/blog/website-speed-fixes">fixing a slow website</a> walks through the main causes.</p>
        <h2>Keep content readable</h2>
        <p>Use a body text size of at least 16 pixels so visitors never need to zoom. Keep paragraphs short, because a paragraph that looks like three lines on a desktop can fill a whole phone screen. Use clear headings so people can scan. Ensure strong contrast between text and background, especially for people reading outdoors in bright sunlight. Simplify navigation into a clear menu with only the most important pages.</p>
        <h2>Forms and checkout need extra care</h2>
        <p>Typing on a phone is tedious. Ask only for information you truly need. Use the correct input types so phones show a number pad for phone numbers and an email keyboard for email addresses. Enable autofill. Show errors right beside the field, in plain words, and keep what the visitor has already typed. For many local businesses, offering a Messenger or click-to-call option alongside the form captures leads who would otherwise give up.</p>
        <h2>Pop-ups, menus and other mobile traps</h2>
        <p>Some things that look harmless on a desktop are a real problem on a phone. A pop-up that fills a large screen with a small box can cover the whole page on mobile, and the close button may be hard to reach. Google has long warned against intrusive pop-ups that block content on mobile. If you use one, keep it small and easy to dismiss.</p>
        <p>Wide tables and long image sliders also struggle on narrow screens, so turn tables into stacked rows. Use a simple, clearly labelled menu, and do not put it inside anything that scrolls separately. If you embed a map or video, check that it does not trap the visitor's thumb as they try to scroll past it.</p>
        <h2>How to test your own site</h2>
        <p>Do not rely on shrinking your desktop browser window. Test on real phones, both a recent model and an older budget Android, using mobile data rather than Wi-Fi. Try to complete your most important task, such as booking, buying or enquiring, using only your thumb. Every point of friction you feel, your customers feel too.</p>
        <p>Then run your main pages through PageSpeed Insights, which tests the mobile version by default. Finally, ask someone who has never used your site to find your prices and your phone number while you watch. Do not help. Where they hesitate is where you need to improve.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>Is mobile-first the same as responsive design?</strong> Not quite. Responsive describes how a site adapts to screens. Mobile-first describes the order you plan and build it in. A good site today is usually both.</p>
        <p><strong>Do I need a separate mobile app?</strong> For most small businesses, no. A well-built mobile website is enough. An app makes sense when customers return often and need features a website cannot offer.</p>
        <p><strong>How do I know if my website is mobile-friendly?</strong> Test it on real phones and run PageSpeed Insights. If you have to zoom, if buttons are hard to tap or if pages take a long time to load, it needs work.</p>
        <p>Every website built through our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> is built mobile-first, and you test the whole site on a staging link before it goes live.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'small-business-website-checklist',
      title: "Small Business Website Checklist: 15 Must-Haves Before You Launch",
      category: 'Development',
      date: '06 Jun 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Justin Sumaya',
      authorRole: 'Head of Development',
      authorPhoto: 'https://fazeddigital.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/151652330_3908937289163068_8338977159966182887_n-450x450.jpg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1702360174953-b9d72d5ddba2?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Launching a new business website? Use this 15-point checklist covering pages, SEO, speed, trust and tracking to make sure nothing important is missed.",
      content: `
        <p>Before you launch a small business website, check 15 things across five areas: essential pages, trust and conversion, SEO foundations, speed, mobile and security, and tracking. Most take minutes to confirm, and each one prevents a problem that could quietly cost you customers for months. Work through this list whether you built the site yourself or hired an agency.</p>
        <p>A launch is exciting, and that excitement is exactly when details get missed. Contact forms sending to an old email address, no analytics installed and pages still blocked from Google are all easy mistakes to make. Take the list one item at a time, and give each a real test instead of assuming it works.</p>
        <h2>Essential pages</h2>
        <p><strong>1. A clear homepage</strong> that states what you do, who you serve and what visitors should do next, all visible without scrolling. Ask a stranger to explain your business after five seconds. If they cannot, rewrite the top of the page.</p>
        <p><strong>2. Individual service pages</strong> for each main offering, rather than one long page listing everything. Each page gives you a chance to appear for a specific search and to answer that customer's questions.</p>
        <p><strong>3. An about page</strong> with real photos of you and your team, because people buy from people. Avoid stock photos of smiling strangers. Say who you are, how long you have been doing this and why customers should trust you.</p>
        <p><strong>4. A contact page</strong> with a form, phone number, email, address, embedded map and business hours. Our guide to <a href="/blog/contact-page-design">contact page design</a> covers what makes one work.</p>
        <p><strong>5. Privacy policy and terms.</strong> If you collect personal data through forms, you will usually need a privacy policy, and customers and advertising platforms expect one. The rules depend on where you and your customers are, so check what applies to you, or ask a lawyer.</p>
        <h2>Trust and conversion</h2>
        <p><strong>6. Social proof</strong> such as testimonials, reviews, client logos or case studies, placed near your calls to action. Only use real ones, with permission.</p>
        <p><strong>7. A clear call to action on every page</strong>, so no page is a dead end. Check that each one says what happens next, such as "Get a free quote" rather than "Submit".</p>
        <p><strong>8. Working forms.</strong> Submit every form yourself and confirm the email arrives in the right inbox, not in spam. Test the confirmation message too. This one matters more than any other, because a broken form loses enquiries in silence.</p>
        <p><strong>9. A professional email address</strong> on your own domain, which looks far more credible than a free webmail address. Send a test message to a Gmail account to make sure your emails do not land in spam.</p>
        <h2>SEO foundations</h2>
        <p><strong>10. Unique title tags and meta descriptions</strong> on every page, written to include the page's main keyword. Search your site with "site:yourdomain.com" a few days after launch to see how Google shows them.</p>
        <p><strong>11. Search Console and a sitemap.</strong> Verify your site in Google Search Console, submit your XML sitemap and double-check that no page is accidentally set to "noindex", a common leftover from development. Check that your robots.txt file is not blocking the site too.</p>
        <p><strong>12. Correct heading structure</strong>, with one H1 per page and logical H2s beneath it, plus descriptive alt text on images. Our <a href="/blog/on-page-seo-checklist">on-page SEO checklist</a> goes through these in more detail.</p>
        <h2>Speed, mobile and security</h2>
        <p><strong>13. Mobile testing on real devices.</strong> Check every page on at least one iPhone and one Android phone. Tap every button, open every menu and fill in every form.</p>
        <p><strong>14. Performance and SSL.</strong> Run PageSpeed Insights and fix major issues, especially oversized images. Confirm your SSL certificate is active so the site loads with HTTPS and no browser warnings, and that the non-www and www versions redirect to a single address.</p>
        <h2>Tracking and measurement</h2>
        <p><strong>15. Analytics and conversion tracking.</strong> Install an analytics tool before launch, not after, so you have data from day one. Set up events for the actions that matter: form submissions, phone clicks, bookings and purchases. Without this, you will never know which pages and channels actually bring in business. If you run ads or use analytics cookies, check whether you need a consent notice for visitors in your market.</p>
        <h2>Who should own what</h2>
        <p>There is one more check that does not fit on the list because it is about you rather than the site. Before launch, confirm you have your own login for the domain, the hosting, the website admin, Google Analytics, Search Console and your Google Business Profile. If an agency or freelancer set these up, make sure the accounts are in your name, or that you are the owner. Save the details somewhere safe, and set the domain and SSL to auto-renew. It is much easier to sort this out now than after someone has moved on.</p>
        <h2>After launch: the first 30 days</h2>
        <p>Launch is the beginning, not the end. In the first month, monitor Search Console for crawl errors, watch analytics to see which pages visitors use and where they leave, ask your first customers for feedback, and request <a href="/blog/get-more-google-reviews">Google reviews</a>. Small adjustments in the first few weeks, based on real behaviour, can make a big difference. If you replaced an old site, also check that your old URLs redirect properly and that nothing important now shows a 404 page.</p>
        <h2>Print it, tick it, launch it</h2>
        <p>Go through the list one item at a time and do not launch until every box is ticked. If you are short of time, do items 8, 11 and 15 first. Those three are the ones that fail silently.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>What pages does a small business website need?</strong> At minimum: a homepage, a page for each main service, an about page, a contact page and a privacy policy.</p>
        <p><strong>How long does a pre-launch check take?</strong> For a small site, a few focused hours is usually enough. Allow extra time for fixing whatever you find, so test before your launch day.</p>
        <p>Prefer to hand this over? Our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> includes a Day 6 review on a staging link, so you can test the whole site before it goes live, and we hand everything over to you at launch.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'why-brand-identity-matters',
      title: 'Why Brand Identity Is Your Most Important Business Asset',
      category: 'Branding',
      date: '18 Apr 2025',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Lord Arcamo',
      authorRole: 'Founder',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-lord.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1434626881859-194d67b2b86f?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "In a market flooded with options, the brands that win are the ones people remember. Here's why investing in identity is always worth it.",
      content: `
        <p>Brand identity matters because it makes your business easy to recognise, remember and trust. For a small business, that is one of the few assets that keeps working after an ad stops running. It does not replace a good product, but it helps a good product get chosen instead of overlooked.</p>
        <p>In a crowded category, the brand that wins is not always the best product. It is often the one people remember when they are ready to buy. Brand identity is the system that makes remembering easy.</p>
        <h2>What brand identity actually is</h2>
        <p>Brand identity is not just a logo. It is the complete visual and verbal language your business uses: your logo, typography, colour palette, photography style and tone of voice, and the way they work together everywhere customers meet you.</p>
        <p>It helps to separate three things that often get mixed up. Your <strong>brand</strong> is what people think and say about you. Your <strong>brand identity</strong> is what you deliberately show them. Your <strong>logo</strong> is one part of that identity. You cannot fully control the first, but you have complete control over the second, and it shapes the first over time.</p>
        <p>When done well, identity builds recognition and trust, and it makes each marketing peso or dollar work harder. When done poorly, or not at all, you start from zero every time someone comes across you.</p>
        <h2>The business case, honestly</h2>
        <p>You will see a lot of big numbers quoted about branding, so treat them with care. One of the most repeated comes from a 2016 survey by Lucidpress and Demand Metric, where more than 200 marketing and brand leaders estimated that consistent branding could add 23% to revenue. That is an estimate from people who work in branding, in a report from a company that sells branding software. It is not a measured result, and it will not predict yours.</p>
        <p>The stronger argument is simple logic, and you can check it against your own business. A recognisable business needs less explaining. A business that looks established can usually charge more than one that looks improvised. A customer who remembers your name can find you again without you paying for the click. And a clear identity makes every flyer, post and proposal faster to produce, because the decisions are already made.</p>
        <h2>The compounding effect</h2>
        <p>The real power of brand identity is that it compounds. Every piece of content, every ad and every email that carries your brand consistently adds to a mental bank account in your audience's mind. Over months and years, this builds the kind of recognition that makes people choose you with less comparison.</p>
        <p>The reverse is also true. If your Facebook page, invoice, shopfront and website all look like different companies, none of those impressions add up. You are paying to be seen and then asking people to start again each time.</p>
        <p>That is why getting the foundation right early is such good value. It costs far less to set up a system once than to fix years of mismatched materials later.</p>
        <h2>What good identity looks like in a real project</h2>
        <p>Cleen &amp; Green is a premium cleaning company in the Philippines. They wanted to sit above the typical cleaning service, but their old brand and website did not show the eco-conscious service they actually delivered. We built a full identity around those values: a fresh green palette, a clean type system and a clear visual language, carried across a new WordPress site. You can read <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/work/02">how the Cleen &amp; Green project came together</a>.</p>
        <p>The lesson is that the design followed the positioning. The green was not picked because it looked nice. It was picked because it said what the business stood for.</p>
        <h2>A quick test for your own brand</h2>
        <p>Lay out everything your business uses on a table or a screen: your logo files, website, social profiles, invoices, signage, quotes and uniforms. Then ask four questions. Would a stranger know these all came from the same business? Do they say what you do and who you serve? Do they match the quality of your actual work? Would you be proud to hand them to your ideal customer?</p>
        <p>If you answered no to one or two, some tidying up may do the job. If you answered no to most of them, you probably need a proper rebuild.</p>
        <h2>Where to start</h2>
        <p>If your identity is inconsistent or no longer reflects who you are, the answer is not to keep patching. Start with strategy: who are you, who do you serve, what do you believe and what makes you different? From that foundation, design can speak clearly. Then write the decisions down in a <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/blog/brand-style-guide">brand style guide</a> so that everyone who touches your brand gets it right.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>Do small businesses really need brand identity?</strong> Yes, though it can be simple. A clear logo, two fonts, a small colour palette and a consistent tone of voice will do more for you than an expensive campaign run on mismatched materials.</p>
        <p><strong>What is the difference between a logo and a brand identity?</strong> A logo is one mark. A brand identity is the full system around it, including colours, type, imagery, voice and the rules for using them.</p>
        <p><strong>When should I invest in it?</strong> Ideally before you spend heavily on marketing, because everything you promote will carry that identity. It is also worth doing when you move upmarket or your materials no longer match your work.</p>
        <p>Ready to make your business look as good as it is? <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/contact">Tell us about your brand</a> and we will talk through what it would take.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'core-web-vitals-seo',
      title: 'Core Web Vitals: What They Are and Why They Destroy Rankings',
      category: 'SEO',
      date: '02 Apr 2025',
      readTime: '4 min read',
      author: 'Russel Heyrana',
      authorRole: 'Marketing Director',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-russel.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1460925895917-afdab827c52f?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Google's page experience signals are now a confirmed ranking factor. We break down LCP, FID, and CLS in plain English — and how to fix them.",
      content: `
        <p>Core Web Vitals are three Google measurements of how a page feels to real visitors: how fast it loads, how quickly it responds and how steady it stays. They are a real ranking signal, but a modest one. Poor scores rarely destroy rankings alone, yet they do cost you visitors and close contests.</p>
        <p>Google began using them in its ranking systems in 2021. Performance problems can go unnoticed for years, because a site usually feels fine to the person who owns it, on a good phone and a fast office connection. Your customers are not always so lucky.</p>
        <h2>The three metrics that matter</h2>
        <p><strong>Largest Contentful Paint (LCP)</strong> measures how long the main content of a page takes to load. Google's target is under 2.5 seconds. Common culprits are unoptimised images, slow server response and render-blocking CSS and JavaScript.</p>
        <p><strong>Interaction to Next Paint (INP)</strong> measures responsiveness: how quickly your page reacts when someone taps or clicks. Google's target is under 200 milliseconds. INP replaced First Input Delay (FID) in March 2024, so older guides that mention FID are out of date. Long tasks on the browser's main thread are usually the problem.</p>
        <p><strong>Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS)</strong> measures visual stability: does your content jump around as it loads? Google's target is under 0.1. If you have ever tapped the wrong button because an ad pushed the page down, you have felt a bad CLS score.</p>
        <h2>Do they really destroy rankings?</h2>
        <p>Not usually on their own, and it is worth being honest about this. Google's own guidance says it still aims to show the most relevant content even when the page experience is poor. It also says good Core Web Vitals scores do not guarantee top rankings.</p>
        <p>So a slow page with the best answer can still rank. Where speed counts is when several pages answer the question equally well, which is common in competitive local searches. Page experience can then help decide who comes out ahead.</p>
        <p>The bigger cost is usually not the ranking at all. A visitor who waits, mis-taps or gives up on a slow page is a lost enquiry, wherever you rank. That is the real reason to fix these numbers. Treat them as a customer problem first and an SEO problem second.</p>
        <h2>How to measure them</h2>
        <p>Use Google's PageSpeed Insights, the Core Web Vitals report in Search Console, or the Web Vitals Chrome extension. You will see two kinds of data, and the difference matters.</p>
        <p><strong>Field data</strong> comes from real Chrome users visiting your site over the previous 28 days. This is what Google uses to judge your pages, so fix it first. <strong>Lab data</strong> is a single simulated test. It is useful for finding causes and for checking a fix immediately, but it is not what visitors actually experienced.</p>
        <p>Small sites often have no field data at all, because they do not get enough visits. If so, use the lab results as your guide, and test your homepage and your most important service pages on a real mid-range phone.</p>
        <p>Remember that field data changes slowly. After a fix, the lab score moves straight away, while the field numbers can take about a month to catch up.</p>
        <h2>Quick wins for each metric</h2>
        <p><strong>For LCP:</strong> compress and properly size images, and use a modern format such as WebP. Do not lazy-load the main image at the top of the page, because that delays the very thing being measured. Use a CDN and decent hosting. Our guide to <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/blog/image-optimisation-small-business">image optimisation</a> covers the details.</p>
        <p><strong>For INP:</strong> cut third-party scripts. Analytics, chat widgets and ad platforms are often the biggest culprits. Load the ones you keep with defer or async, and remove any plugin you no longer use.</p>
        <p><strong>For CLS:</strong> set explicit width and height on images and embeds so the browser can reserve the space. Avoid inserting banners above existing content after the page has loaded. Load web fonts carefully, so the text does not jump when they arrive.</p>
        <h2>Where to start</h2>
        <p>Do not chase a perfect 100. Go to the Search Console report and find the groups of pages marked "Poor". Fix the issue that affects the most important pages first, starting with the pages that bring in enquiries. Check them again, then move on to pages marked "Needs improvement". If your site has deeper problems, our list of <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/blog/website-speed-fixes">common causes of a slow website</a> will help you find them.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>What is a good Core Web Vitals score?</strong> Google's targets are LCP under 2.5 seconds, INP under 200 milliseconds and CLS under 0.1, measured on real visits.</p>
        <p><strong>Is a high PageSpeed score the same as passing Core Web Vitals?</strong> No. The big score at the top is a lab result. Passing depends on the field data from real users, so a site can score well in one and poorly in the other.</p>
        <p><strong>Why does my site show no Core Web Vitals data?</strong> Most likely it does not have enough Chrome visits to report on. That is normal for new or small sites. Use the lab test in PageSpeed Insights until it does.</p>
        <p>Want a second pair of eyes on your numbers? <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/audit">Book a free 15-minute website check</a> and we will go through your site with you on Google Meet.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'astro-vs-nextjs-agencies',
      title: 'Astro vs Next.js: Which Should Your Agency Choose in 2025?',
      category: 'Development',
      date: '21 Mar 2025',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Justin Sumaya',
      authorRole: 'Head of Development',
      authorPhoto: 'https://fazeddigital.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/151652330_3908937289163068_8338977159966182887_n-450x450.jpg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1461749280684-dccba630e2f6?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "We've built with both. Here's an honest comparison from the trenches — performance, DX, and the projects each framework is truly built for.",
      content: `
        <p>For most marketing sites, blogs and brochure sites, choose Astro. For apps with logins, dashboards and live data, choose Next.js. That is the short answer to Astro vs Next.js, and it holds because the real question is how much of each page actually needs to be interactive.</p>
        <p>We build with both, and this site runs on Astro. Here is our honest take on when to reach for each one, what the choice means for your budget and upkeep, and where the fans of each framework leave things out.</p>
        <h2>The core difference</h2>
        <p>Astro ships zero JavaScript by default. It is built on the "islands" architecture: static HTML, with interactive components only where you actually need them. That suits content-heavy sites, where most of the page is text and images that do not need to respond to anything.</p>
        <p>Next.js is a full React framework with flexible rendering: static pages, server rendering, incremental regeneration and client-side rendering. It shines on applications where fresh data, logins and complex interactions matter more than the lightest possible page.</p>
        <p>Put simply, Astro starts with a document and lets you add interactivity. Next.js starts with an application and lets you make parts of it static. Both can reach into the other's territory, but you get the best results when you start from the side closest to your project.</p>
        <h2>When to choose Astro</h2>
        <p>Choose Astro for marketing sites, agency portfolios, blogs, documentation and landing pages, where the main goal is fast loading and good SEO. With so little JavaScript to download, it is easier to hit good <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/blog/core-web-vitals-seo">Core Web Vitals</a> scores, especially on mid-range phones and patchy mobile data.</p>
        <p>It also lets you add interactive pieces without rebuilding the whole site. You can drop in a React, Vue or Svelte component for a pricing calculator or a booking widget, and only that part loads JavaScript. Our own site works this way: the pages are static, and the 3D dot field runs as a separate script that loads on its own.</p>
        <h2>When to choose Next.js</h2>
        <p>Choose Next.js for SaaS dashboards, member areas, large online stores, apps with real-time data, and anything with logins and personalisation. If most screens depend on who is signed in, you would otherwise end up rebuilding much of what Next.js already gives you.</p>
        <p>The React ecosystem is its other big strength. React developers are easy to hire, most third-party tools offer React components, and Vercel, the company behind Next.js, provides a very smooth hosting path for it.</p>
        <h2>What the choice means for your budget and upkeep</h2>
        <p><strong>Hosting.</strong> A static Astro site is plain files, so it can sit on low-cost static hosting and has very little to go wrong. Next.js can also be exported as static pages, but its server features need a platform or a Node server that supports them, which adds cost and setup.</p>
        <p><strong>Security and maintenance.</strong> A static site has no database or admin login exposed to the public, so there is less to attack. A Next.js app with logins and a database needs more care. That is not a flaw. It is the normal cost of running an application.</p>
        <p><strong>Content editing.</strong> Neither framework gives your client an admin panel on its own. With either one, you connect a headless CMS if non-technical people need to edit pages. If your client wants to edit everything themselves with no developer involved, compare both options with <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/blog/wordpress-vs-custom-build">WordPress or a custom build</a> before deciding.</p>
        <p><strong>Your team.</strong> A React team will feel at home in Next.js straight away. Astro is quick to learn because its components look a lot like HTML. If your developers only know one of them, include that learning time in your quote.</p>
        <h2>The trade-offs fans leave out</h2>
        <p>Astro is not the right tool when interactivity spreads across the whole page. Once every section needs shared state and live data, you end up managing lots of separate islands, and a single React app would be simpler.</p>
        <p>Next.js is often more than a brochure site needs. It works, but you are shipping and maintaining a React application to display content that rarely changes. It also changes quickly, so a site you leave alone for two years may take real work to upgrade.</p>
        <h2>A quick test for your next project</h2>
        <p>Ask five questions. Do users need to log in? Does most of the content change per user? Do pages depend on live data? Is the project closer to a tool than a publication? Will it need a large React codebase? If you answer yes to two or more, start with Next.js. If you answer no to all or most of them, start with Astro.</p>
        <h2>The honest verdict</h2>
        <p>For most agency client sites, we reach for Astro first because the performance baseline is simply better with less effort. For anything with significant app functionality, Next.js is still a strong choice. The framework will not save a weak site on its own, though. Good content, clear structure and careful images still do most of the work.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>Is Astro better than Next.js for SEO?</strong> Both can produce pages Google reads well. Astro's advantage is that it makes fast pages the default, while Next.js needs more care to keep JavaScript under control.</p>
        <p><strong>Can I use React with Astro?</strong> Yes. Astro supports React components as islands, so you can reuse them without making the whole site a React app.</p>
        <p><strong>Can a site move from one to the other later?</strong> Yes, but it is a rebuild of the templates. Your content and designs carry over more easily than the code, so choose based on what the project needs in the next two to three years.</p>
        <p>Weighing up the stack for a custom project? <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/contact">Tell us what you are building</a> and we will recommend the right fit, not just the one we like best.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'ux-mistakes-killing-conversions',
      title: '7 UX Mistakes That Are Quietly Killing Your Conversion Rate',
      category: 'Design',
      date: '05 Mar 2025',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Lord Arcamo',
      authorRole: 'Founder',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-lord.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1581291518857-4d27a540d9f2?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Most conversion problems aren't about copy or offers — they're about friction. Here are the design errors we see most often in audits.",
      content: `
        <p>The UX mistakes that most often hurt your conversion rate are unclear priorities, long forms, poor mobile use, slow pages, hidden proof, inconsistent design and missing feedback. All seven are forms of friction: small obstacles between a willing visitor and your enquiry form. The good news is that most can be fixed without a full redesign.</p>
        <p>A weak offer is not always the reason a website underperforms. Before you change your prices or rewrite your whole pitch, make sure people can actually get through the page. Here is what to look for, and what to do about each problem.</p>
        <h2>1. No clear visual hierarchy</h2>
        <p>Users scan, they do not read. If your page has no clear primary action, the one thing you most want visitors to do, they will default to doing nothing. Every page needs a single, unmistakable call to action that stands above the rest.</p>
        <p><strong>The fix:</strong> squint at your page, or blur a screenshot. The most important thing should still stand out. If three buttons compete, pick one and make the others quieter text links.</p>
        <h2>2. Forms that ask too much</h2>
        <p>Each extra field is one more reason to stop. Ask only for what you actually need at this stage of the relationship. Name and email or phone is often enough to start. Everything else can wait until the first conversation.</p>
        <p><strong>The fix:</strong> take each field and ask what you do with that answer before you reply. If the answer is nothing, remove it. If you need detail to give a quote, explain why, and mark optional fields clearly. Our guide to <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/blog/contact-page-design">contact page design</a> goes further.</p>
        <h2>3. Mobile-hostile interactions</h2>
        <p>Hover states that do not work on touch. Tiny tap targets. Horizontal scrolling. These are not minor annoyances. They actively drive people away, and for many small businesses most of the traffic is on phones. Check your own split in Google Analytics.</p>
        <p><strong>The fix:</strong> Apple's design guidelines recommend tap targets of at least 44 points, so use that as your minimum for main buttons. Put a real phone in your hand and try to complete your main task using one thumb. Anything hidden behind a hover needs another way in.</p>
        <h2>4. Slow loading on real devices</h2>
        <p>Your site feels fast on your office connection and your new laptop. Try it on a mid-range Android phone on mobile data. Then look at how mobile visitors behave in your analytics compared with desktop visitors. A big gap is a useful warning sign.</p>
        <p><strong>The fix:</strong> start with images, which are usually the heaviest part of the page, then remove scripts you no longer use. Our guide to <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/blog/website-speed-fixes">why websites run slowly</a> covers the seven common causes.</p>
        <h2>5. Trust signals buried or absent</h2>
        <p>Testimonials, case studies, client logos and certifications should be visible before you ask for commitment. Placing them after your call to action is too late, and placing them on a separate page means most people will never see them.</p>
        <p><strong>The fix:</strong> put one piece of real proof near each main button. Use real names and real work. If you are new and do not have reviews yet, show your process, your team and your guarantee instead. Never invent proof, because customers can usually tell, and it can land you in legal trouble.</p>
        <h2>6. Inconsistent design language</h2>
        <p>When buttons look different on different pages, when spacing is arbitrary and when colours feel random, people sense a lack of care. It erodes trust before they have read a word. This usually creeps in over time, as pages get added by different people.</p>
        <p><strong>The fix:</strong> choose one button style, one heading style and a small set of colours, then go through your site page by page and bring everything back in line. It is dull work, and it makes a site feel noticeably more solid.</p>
        <h2>7. No feedback on interaction</h2>
        <p>What happens when I click this button? Did the form submit? Did my cart update? Loading indicators, success messages and clear error explanations are not optional extras. Without them, people click twice, give up or assume your site is broken.</p>
        <p><strong>The fix:</strong> test every form with a wrong entry and with a correct one. Error messages should say exactly what to change, and they should keep what the person has already typed. After a successful submission, tell people what happens next and when you will reply.</p>
        <h2>How to find these problems on your own site</h2>
        <p>You are too close to your own website to see them, so use other people's eyes. Ask three to five people who have never seen your site to complete one task, such as requesting a quote, while you watch without helping. Jakob Nielsen's usability research has long argued that a handful of testers will find most of the serious problems.</p>
        <p>Then add data. Google Analytics 4 shows where people leave, and a free tool such as Microsoft Clarity records anonymised sessions so you can watch where they get stuck. Fix one thing at a time, so you can tell what made a difference.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>What should I fix first?</strong> Start with your highest-traffic page that asks for an action, then work through the problems that stop someone finishing that action. Speed, mobile use and your form usually come first.</p>
        <p><strong>Do I need a redesign to fix these mistakes?</strong> Often not. Forms, buttons, proof and messages can usually be fixed on your current site. If the problems run through every template, a rebuild may be better value.</p>
        <p>Want someone to walk through your site with fresh eyes? <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/audit">Book a free 15-minute website check</a> and we will show you where visitors are likely to get stuck.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'content-strategy-small-business',
      title: 'A No-Nonsense Content Strategy for Small Businesses',
      category: 'Strategy',
      date: '14 Feb 2025',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Reancirl Balaba',
      authorRole: 'Co-Founder',
      authorPhoto: 'https://fazeddigital.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/117749302_4540601115965165_6039017566881807039_n-450x450.jpg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1552664730-d307ca884978?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "You don't need a 40-page strategy document. You need to know what to say, to whom, on which channels, and how often. Let's simplify.",
      content: `
        <p>A content strategy for a small business is a short written answer to four questions: who you are talking to, what they need to know, where you will say it, and how often you can keep it up. You do not need a 40-page document. One page you actually follow is enough.</p>
        <p>Most small business content plans fail not because they were badly designed, but because nobody carried them out. The plan was too complex, too expensive or too far from the real work of the business. So build yours around what a busy owner can sustain while still serving customers.</p>
        <h2>The only four questions you need to answer</h2>
        <p><strong>Who are you talking to?</strong> Not "everyone." Pick one specific type of person with a specific problem. "Homeowners in Cebu planning a kitchen renovation" gives you something to write about. "People who like nice homes" does not. If you serve two very different groups, choose the one that brings in the most profitable work and start there.</p>
        <p><strong>What do they actually care about?</strong> Not what you care about. Think about what they are worried about, what they type into Google, and what decision they are trying to make. Your content answers those questions. If you are unsure, read your last 20 customer emails or Messenger chats. The same handful of questions will keep coming up.</p>
        <p><strong>What channels do they use?</strong> Do not try to be everywhere. One blog plus one social channel, done consistently, beats five channels done badly. A business that sells to other businesses may do better on LinkedIn and email. A restaurant or salon will usually do better on Facebook and Instagram. Go where your buyers already spend time, not where marketing articles say you should be.</p>
        <p><strong>What can you sustainably produce?</strong> One good piece of content every two weeks beats a burst of daily posts followed by silence. Before you commit, look at your calendar and be honest. Build a rhythm you can keep up for two years, not two months.</p>
        <h2>The simple system: one main piece a month</h2>
        <p>Publish one long-form piece of content each month, such as a blog post, video or podcast episode, that answers a genuine question your ideal customer has. Then cut it down. That one piece gives you four social posts, one email to your list and a dozen short stories or updates.</p>
        <p>This works because the thinking happens once. You are not starting from a blank page every time you need a Facebook post. You are pulling out one tip, one example or one common mistake from something you have already written.</p>
        <h2>What to write about first</h2>
        <p>Start with the questions people ask just before they buy. These bring in fewer readers than broad topics, but the readers they bring are much closer to hiring you. Five types of question work for almost any business.</p>
        <p><strong>Cost:</strong> what does this usually cost, and what changes the price? <strong>Process:</strong> what happens after I say yes, and how long will it take? <strong>Comparison:</strong> should I choose this option or that one? <strong>Problems:</strong> why does this keep happening, and can I fix it myself? <strong>Fit:</strong> is this right for a business like mine?</p>
        <p>Write five questions under each heading and you have a list of 25 topics, which is two years of content at one piece a month. To check how people phrase those questions, use our guide to <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/blog/keyword-research-small-business">keyword research for small businesses</a>.</p>
        <h2>What a month looks like in practice</h2>
        <p>Say you run a small dental clinic and this month's question is "how much do braces cost, and what changes the price?" In week one, you write the article with your dentist's input. In week two, you share a short post on the three things that affect the price, with a link to the article. In week three, you post an answer to the most common follow-up question and send the article to your email list. In week four, you share one more tip and pick next month's topic.</p>
        <p>That is about half a day of writing and an hour of scheduling. Block the time in your calendar on the same day each month, and treat it like a customer appointment.</p>
        <h2>How to know if it is working</h2>
        <p>Do not judge a content plan by likes. Check three things every month. In Google Search Console, see whether your articles are starting to appear for the questions you wrote about. In <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/blog/google-analytics-4-small-business">Google Analytics 4</a>, see whether readers go on to your contact or service pages. And ask every new enquiry how they found you, then write it down.</p>
        <p>Give it time. Search traffic usually builds slowly, so judge the plan after six months, not six weeks. If a topic brings in enquiries, write more around it. If nothing has moved after six months, the questions are probably wrong, not the idea of publishing.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>How often should a small business post content?</strong> As often as you can keep up without stopping. For most owners that means one main piece a month and one or two short posts a week. Consistency matters more than volume.</p>
        <p><strong>Do I need a blog if I already post on Facebook?</strong> It helps. Social posts fade within days, while a useful article on your own website can keep being found through Google for years. Use social media to share it, and your website to keep it.</p>
        <p><strong>Can I use AI to write my content?</strong> You can use it to draft and organise, but your real examples, prices and opinions are what make content worth reading. Check every fact, and do not publish anything you would not say to a customer.</p>
        <p>Not sure your website is ready to turn readers into enquiries? <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/audit">Book a free 15-minute website check</a> on Google Meet and we will show you what to fix first.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'typography-web-design',
      title: 'Typography in Web Design: The Rules That Actually Matter',
      category: 'Design',
      date: '28 Jan 2025',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Lord Arcamo',
      authorRole: 'Founder',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-lord.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1524781289445-ddf8f5695861?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Type is 95% of web design, yet it's the most overlooked element. The principles that separate good typography from great — and from unreadable.",
      content: `
        <p>Good web typography comes down to a few rules that matter: a clear size scale, comfortable line length, generous spacing, strong contrast and no more than two fonts. Get those right and your site reads as professional, even with a simple layout. Get them wrong and no colour or imagery will save it.</p>
        <p>Oliver Reichenstein famously wrote that web design is 95% typography. After years of design work, we think he had a point. Most of what people do on a website is read, so the type is the product. Here is what to check on your own site, with numbers you can hand straight to your designer.</p>
        <h2>The scale problem</h2>
        <p>Most websites use too small a type scale. If your body copy is 14px and your headings are 22px, you do not have a hierarchy. You have noise. A clear scale creates visual order without any extra effort.</p>
        <p>Start with 16px body text, then multiply by a fixed ratio of at least 1.25 for each step up. That gives you roughly 16, 20, 25, 31 and 39 pixels. Use a larger ratio for a bolder look, and let your largest headings shrink on phones so a long word does not break the layout. Limit yourself to four or five sizes across the whole site, and write them down so that new pages stay consistent. For more on how size directs attention, see our guide to <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/blog/visual-hierarchy-web-design">visual hierarchy</a>.</p>
        <h2>Line length is non-negotiable</h2>
        <p>Robert Bringhurst's The Elements of Typographic Style gives 45 to 75 characters as the comfortable range for a line of text. Count yours. If your text column spans the full width of a desktop screen, you are making reading hard work, because the eye loses its place on the way back to the start of the next line.</p>
        <p>The fix is one line of CSS. Set a max-width on your text column, and a value of around 65ch is a good starting point. On a phone, the screen width keeps lines short for you, so check the opposite problem there: large headings that break into one or two words per line.</p>
        <h2>Pairing fonts: the one rule</h2>
        <p>Pair a serif with a sans-serif, or use two sans-serifs with clearly different weights and personalities. The mistake is pairing fonts that are too similar, because they compete rather than complement each other. Contrast is the entire point.</p>
        <p>If you are not sure, use one good family in two or three weights. Plenty of strong brands do exactly that. Two fonts is the sensible limit for most small business sites. Each extra one adds download weight and makes the design look less considered.</p>
        <p>When choosing, test real sentences from your site, not the sample text on the font page. Check that the numbers look right if you show prices, and that the font includes every character you need, such as the peso sign. Some typefaces leave it out.</p>
        <h2>Whitespace is type's best friend</h2>
        <p>Give your text more space between lines, more space between paragraphs and more padding around blocks. For body text, a line height of 1.5 to 1.7 is a good range. Headings need less, usually about 1.1 to 1.3, because large type with loose lines falls apart.</p>
        <p>Whitespace is not waste. It is the room type needs to breathe. Keep space above a heading larger than the space below it, so the heading clearly belongs to the text it introduces. Compressed text communicates cheapness, whether you intend it or not.</p>
        <h2>Contrast and readability on real screens</h2>
        <p>A beautiful font in pale grey is still unreadable. The Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) ask for a contrast ratio of at least 4.5:1 for normal body text and 3:1 for large text. Free contrast checkers will test your colours in seconds.</p>
        <p>Then look at your site on a phone outdoors, because that is where many of your customers will read it. Thin font weights and light grey text fail first in bright light. If you have to squint, change the weight or the colour.</p>
        <h2>Variable fonts are worth using</h2>
        <p>Modern variable fonts give you any weight and width from a single file, which can replace several separate downloads and allows smooth changes in weight. Browsers support them well. They pay off most when you use three or more weights. If you only need regular and bold, two small static files may be lighter.</p>
        <p>Whatever you choose, host your fonts sensibly. Load only the weights you use, use the WOFF2 format, and set font-display so text appears straight away rather than staying invisible. Also check the licence. Google Fonts are free for commercial use, but many others charge separately for web use.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>What is the best font size for a website?</strong> Use at least 16px for body text on all screens. Go higher if your typeface looks small or your readers are older. Do not drop below it for main content on mobile.</p>
        <p><strong>How many fonts should a website use?</strong> One or two. If you add a third, it should have a specific job, such as code or prices. Record your choices in a <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/blog/brand-style-guide">brand style guide</a> so everyone uses them the same way.</p>
        <p><strong>Do fonts affect website speed?</strong> Yes. Every font file is a download. Fewer weights, WOFF2 files and sensible loading settings keep the cost small.</p>
        <p>Wondering how your site reads on a phone? <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/audit">Book a free 15-minute website check</a> and we will look at it with you on Google Meet.</p>
      `,
    },
  ];
  return posts.map(p => ({ params: { slug: p.slug }, props: p }));
}

// ── everything below is template code (the array above is the nightly routine's contract) ──
const { title, category, date, readTime, author, authorRole, authorPhoto: photoField, excerpt, content, slug } = Astro.props;
const photo = authorPhoto(author, photoField);
// content: normalise apex links to the www host, give every H2 an id for the TOC
const toc: { id: string; text: string }[] = [];
const used = new Set<string>();
const html = String(content)
  .replaceAll('https://fazeddigital.com', 'https://www.fazeddigital.com')
  .replace(/<h2>(.*?)<\/h2>/g, (_m, inner) => {
    const text = String(inner).replace(/<[^>]+>/g, '');
    let id = text.toLowerCase().replace(/&[a-z]+;/g, '').replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-').replace(/^-|-$/g, '').slice(0, 60) || 'section';
    while (used.has(id)) id += '-2';
    used.add(id);
    toc.push({ id, text });
    return `<h2 id="${id}">${inner}</h2>`;
  });
const all = (postsIndex as { slug: string; title: string; category: string; date: string; readTime: string }[]);
const sameCat = all.filter((p) => p.category === category && p.slug !== slug);
const idx = all.findIndex((p) => p.slug === slug);
const related = [...sameCat].sort((a, b) => Math.abs(all.indexOf(a) - idx) - Math.abs(all.indexOf(b) - idx)).slice(0, 3);
// contextual links to services, platform pages and case studies (src/data/links.ts; hand-mapped per post, topic rules for new posts)
const fits = postLinks(slug, title, category);
// short title tag when the headline runs long (src/data/blog-seo.ts); the H1 keeps the full headline
const shortTitle = seoTitles[slug] ?? title;
const pageTitle = shortTitle.length > 51 ? shortTitle : shortTitle.length > 44 ? `${shortTitle} | Fazed` : `${shortTitle} | Fazed Digital`;
const iso = new Date(date).toISOString();
const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'Article', headline: title, description: excerpt,
    author: { '@type': 'Person', name: author, jobTitle: authorRole, url: `${SITE_URL}/about#${authorId(author)}`, worksFor: ORG_REF },
    publisher: ORG_REF, datePublished: iso, dateModified: iso,
    image: `${SITE_URL}/og/blog/${slug}.png`, url: `${SITE_URL}/blog/${slug}`,
    mainEntityOfPage: { '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': `${SITE_URL}/blog/${slug}` },
    articleSection: category, keywords: category, inLanguage: 'en',
  },
  crumbs([['Blog', '/blog'], [category, `/blog/category/${catSlug(category)}`], [title, `/blog/${slug}`]]),
];
---
<Base title={pageTitle} description={excerpt} image={`/og/blog/${slug}.png`} formation="none" schema={schema} current="/blog">
  <div class="progress" aria-hidden="true"><span></span></div>
  <article class="post">
    <header class="container p-head">
      <nav class="crumbs mono" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/blog">Blog</a></li><li><a href={`/blog/category/${catSlug(category)}`}>{category}</a></li><li aria-current="page" class="crumb-cur">{title}</li></ol></nav>
      <p class="mono p-meta"><span class="p-cat">{category}</span> · <time datetime={iso}>{date}</time> · {readTime} · by {author}</p>
      <h1 class="t-d2 p-h1">{title}</h1>
      <p class="t-body-l p-deck">{excerpt}</p>
      <div class="p-cover"><BlogCover slug={slug} category={category} size={1200} /></div>
    </header>
    <div class="container p-grid">
      {toc.length > 2 && (
        <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
          <p class="mono">On this page</p>
          <ol>{toc.map((t) => <li><a href={`#${t.id}`}>{t.text}</a></li>)}</ol>
        </nav>
      )}
      <div class="p-body">
        <div class="prose" set:html={html} />
        <RelatedLinks links={fits} heading="Put this guide to work" eyebrow="Where this fits in our work" id="fit-h" />
        <aside class="author" aria-label="About the author">
          {photo ? <img src={photo} alt={`${author}, ${authorRole} at Fazed Digital`} width="64" height="64" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /> : <span class="a-init mono" aria-hidden="true">{initials(author)}</span>}
          <p>Written by <a class="link" href={`/about#${authorId(author)}`}>{author}</a>, {authorRole} at Fazed Digital.</p>
        </aside>
        <OfferCard ph={phPosts.has(slug)} heading="Ready to put this into practice?" body="Get a professional website live in 7 days. It’s custom, mobile-ready and set up for Google from day one, built by the team who wrote this guide." />
      </div>
    </div>
    {related.length > 0 && (
      <section class="container related" aria-labelledby="rel-h">
        <h2 id="rel-h" class="t-h3">More on {category}</h2>
        <ul>{related.map((p) => <li><a href={`/blog/${p.slug}`}><BlogCover slug={p.slug} category={p.category} size={64} /><span class="mono">{p.readTime}</span><span class="rel-t">{p.title}</span></a></li>)}</ul>
        <a class="link" href={`/blog/category/${catSlug(category)}`}>All {category} guides</a>
      </section>
    )}
  </article>
</Base>
<style>
  .progress { position: fixed; top: 0; left: 0; right: 0; height: 2px; z-index: 70; pointer-events: none; }
  .progress span { display: block; height: 100%; transform-origin: 0 50%; transform: scaleX(0); background-image: radial-gradient(circle, var(--lime) 1px, transparent 1.2px); background-size: 4px 2px; }
  @supports (animation-timeline: scroll()) {
    .progress span { animation: grow linear both; animation-timeline: scroll(root); }
  }
  @keyframes grow { to { transform: scaleX(1); } }
  .p-head { padding-top: calc(var(--header-h) + 72px); display: grid; gap: 1.25rem; max-width: 1000px; }
  .crumbs ol { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .25rem .75rem; }
  .crumbs li:not(:last-child)::after { content: '/'; margin-left: .75rem; color: var(--gray-500); }
  .crumbs a { color: var(--gray-300); min-height: 44px; display: inline-flex; align-items: center; }
  .crumbs a:hover { color: var(--lime); }
  .crumb-cur { color: var(--gray-400); max-width: 40ch; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; white-space: nowrap; min-height: 44px; display: inline-block; line-height: 44px; }
  @media (max-width: 767px) { .crumb-cur { display: none; } .crumbs li:nth-last-child(2)::after { display: none; } } /* the H1 sits right below */
  .p-cat { color: var(--lime); }
  .p-h1 { max-width: 22ch; }
  .p-deck { max-width: 60ch; }
  .p-cover { margin-top: 2rem; aspect-ratio: 21 / 9; overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid var(--gray-700); }
  .p-cover :global(svg) { width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; }
  .p-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr); column-gap: var(--gutter); padding-block: 96px var(--section); }
  .toc { grid-column: 1 / span 3; position: sticky; top: calc(var(--header-h) + 24px); align-self: start; display: grid; gap: .75rem; }
  .toc ol { display: grid; gap: .1rem; border-left: 1px solid var(--gray-700); }
  .toc a { display: block; padding: .45rem 0 .45rem 1rem; color: var(--gray-400); font-size: .9rem; line-height: 1.35; margin-left: -1px; border-left: 1px solid transparent; }
  .toc a:hover, .toc a:focus-visible { color: var(--white); border-left-color: var(--lime); }
  .p-body { grid-column: 5 / span 8; display: grid; gap: 3rem; max-width: 64ch; }
  .prose { font-size: var(--fs-article); line-height: 1.7; color: var(--gray-200); hanging-punctuation: first; }
  .prose :global(p + p), .prose :global(p + ul), .prose :global(ul + p), .prose :global(p + ol), .prose :global(ol + p) { margin-top: 1.1em; }
  .prose :global(h2) { font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 2.6vw, 2.2rem); line-height: 1.1; margin: 2.2em 0 .7em; scroll-margin-top: 96px; }
  .prose :global(h3) { font-size: 1.3rem; margin: 1.6em 0 .5em; }
  .prose :global(strong) { color: var(--white); }
  .prose :global(ul), .prose :global(ol) { padding-left: 1.25rem; display: grid; gap: .5em; }
  .prose :global(ul) { list-style: none; }
  .prose :global(ul li) { position: relative; }
  .prose :global(ul li)::before { content: ''; position: absolute; left: -1.1rem; top: .7em; width: 5px; height: 5px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--lime); }
  .prose :global(ol) { list-style: decimal; }
  .prose :global(a) { color: var(--white); text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-color: var(--gray-500); text-underline-offset: .2em; background-image: linear-gradient(var(--lime), var(--lime)); background-size: 0 100%; background-repeat: no-repeat; transition: background-size .25s, color .2s; }
  .prose :global(a:hover), .prose :global(a:focus-visible) { background-size: 100% 100%; color: var(--black); }
  .prose :global(blockquote) { border-left: 2px solid var(--lime); padding-left: 1.25rem; color: var(--white); margin: 1.5em 0; }
  .author { display: flex; gap: 1rem; align-items: center; }
  .author img, .a-init { width: 64px; height: 64px; border-radius: 50%; object-fit: cover; object-position: top; flex: none; }
  .a-init { border: 1.5px solid var(--lime); color: var(--lime); display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; }
  .related { padding-bottom: var(--section); display: grid; gap: 1.5rem; }
  .related ul { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: var(--gutter); }
  .related a:not(.link) { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 64px 1fr; column-gap: 1rem; row-gap: .25rem; padding: 1.25rem; border: 1px solid var(--gray-700); height: 100%; }
  .related a:not(.link) :global(svg) { grid-row: span 2; }
  .related a:not(.link):hover { border-color: var(--lime); }
  .rel-t { color: var(--white); font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.2; }
  .related > .link { justify-self: start; }
  @media (max-width: 1023px) { .toc { display: none; } .p-body { grid-column: 1 / -1; } .related ul { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
</style>
